7e9519cea7\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044d1-982f-7bb2-88b2-9be3d84f45b4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/negative_no_go\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1450\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving second-order differentiation formulas**\",\"**Confirming top coefficient contributions**\",\"**Defining and analyzing operator expansion**\",\"**Summarizing audit status and next steps**\",\"**Analyzing TP theorem rank stability and function space bounds**\",\"**Confirming kernel boundedness and action state structure**\",\"**Bounding coefficients and covariance in ψ2 norm**\",\"**Verifying exponential moment bounds**\",\"**Confirming derivative bounds and tail controls**\",\"**Evaluating norm and derivative bounds**\",\"**Analyzing derivative scaling and norm bounds**\",\"**Testing agent responsiveness**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_z7jy9D3a91QXyapV5067MMFq\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044e6-7490-7ad2-b80f-3c82883a8386\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_cutoff_gns\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1451\",\"summary\":[\"**Investigating trace separation with Hessian expansion**\",\"**Analyzing trace coefficient expansion and cross terms**\",\"**Formalizing GNS action details**\",\"**Establishing Lipschitz bounds and closures**\",\"**Verifying Lipschitz continuity of tail function**\",\"**Verifying tail saturation and uniqueness bounds**\",\"**Confirming integrability and coupling conditions**\",\"**Preparing final theorem affirmation**\",\"**Detailing precise theorem convergence**\",\"**Verifying TP theorem citations and operator norm bounds**\",\"**Refining Gaussian operator norm tail bounds**\",\"**Deriving cutoff ODE constants and concentration bounds**\",\"**Verifying bootstrap exponent bounds and induction step**\",\"**Estimating tail bounds for concentration inequalities**\",\"**Establishing uniform third-derivative bounds**\",\"**Verifying rigorous kernel convergence properties**\",\"**Planning final audit**\",\"**Clarifying hyperparameter dependencies**\",\"**Planning scalar kernel justification**\",\"**Verifying theorem assumptions and citations**\",\"**Proving net existence with finite bounds**\",\"**Integrating bootstrap bounds and confirming uniqueness**\",\"**Analyzing conditional covariance positivity**\",\"**Defining computable time bound T**\\n\\n*\",\"**Stating explicit existence conditions**\",\"**Clarifying uniqueness and derivative definitions**\",\"**Refining derivative bounds and source recurrences**\",\"**Bounding variance and update sums**\",\"**Finalizing theorem outline and plan**\",\"**Outlining proof steps and conventions**\",\"**Verifying action cutoff bounds and tail probabilities**\",\"**Defining V to include y in second derivative calculation**\",\"**Deriving gradient and contraction terms**\",\"**Confirming positive semi-definiteness of matrices**\",\"**Defining kernel and response relations**\",\"**Formalizing kernel expansions and convergence**\",\"**Defining scalar effective kernel \\\\(K_{\\\\rm eff}\\\\)**\",\"**Confirming kernel scalar consistency and citing sources**\",\"**Clarifying error bound notation**\",\"**Finalizing theorem convergence statements**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a044e6-77f1-7961-8e6e-1591e3ed0ae4\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044e6-7490-7ad2-b80f-3c82883a8386\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_cutoff_gns\"},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1452\",\"text\":\"Yes. The affirmative theorem is true for every fixed finite dataset of pairwise-distinct normalized inputs. The missing compact-time bridge can be closed without assuming a nonlinear DMFT theorem.\\n\\nThe natural multi-sample kernel is the full NTK matrix. The proof establishes that this matrix moves—and, more specifically, that its quadratic form in the label direction moves. It does not assert the unrelated statement that its trace must move.\\n\\n## Theorem\\n\\nFix \\\\(p,d<\\\\infty\\\\) and data\\n\\n\\\\[\\nx_1,\\\\ldots,x_p\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^d,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|x_a\\\\|^2=d,\\\\qquad\\nx_a\\\\neq x_b\\\\quad(a\\\\neq b),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith arbitrary labels \\\\(y_a\\\\in\\\\{-1,1\\\\}\\\\).\\n\\nUse\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi(u)=\\\\sin u+\\\\cos u\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the network\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_i^a=w_i^\\\\top x_a,\\\\qquad\\nS_j^a=\\\\sum_{i=1}^n B_{ji}\\\\phi(U_i^a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{n,a}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^n c_j\\\\phi(S_j^a),\\\\qquad\\nL_n=\\\\frac1p\\\\sum_{a=1}^p(f_{n,a}-y_a)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInitialize independently by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nw_i(0)\\\\sim N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1dI_d\\\\right),\\n\\\\qquad\\nB_{ji}(0)\\\\sim N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\frac1n\\\\right),\\n\\\\qquad\\nc_j(0)\\\\sim N(0,n^{-4}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTake\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}=\\\\frac nd,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(2)}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(3)}=\\\\frac1n,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}=e^{-n^{10}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\lambda_n^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nfor all three layers.\\n\\nThere exists\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT_0=\\\\frac1{1536p}>0\\n\\\\]\\n\\nsuch that the piecewise-linear exact-GD interpolation converges in probability, uniformly on every \\\\([0,T]\\\\subset[0,T_0]\\\\), to a unique autonomous action-operator mean-field flow. The convergence includes:\\n\\n- the full current network action state;\\n- both hidden preactivation path laws in \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\);\\n- every parameter-block update;\\n- all three NTK matrices;\\n- output and loss;\\n- both hidden velocity energies.\\n\\nFor each fixed dataset there is \\\\(T_*\\\\in(0,T_0]\\\\) such that, for every \\\\(0<T\\\\le T_*\\\\):\\n\\n- both hidden marginal variances remain finite and positive;\\n- both hidden layers have positive integrated feature speed and nonzero displacement;\\n- all three parameter blocks have positive integrated NTK activity;\\n- the nonlinear regression residual is positive in both hidden layers;\\n- the full NTK matrix is nonconstant;\\n- \\\\(L(T)<L(0)\\\\).\\n\\nNo lower bound is uniform over all distinct datasets; it may deteriorate as two oppositely labelled inputs approach one another.\\n\\n## 1. Exact normalized dynamics\\n\\nSet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC=nc,\\\\qquad\\nH^a=\\\\phi(U^a),\\\\qquad\\nG^a=\\\\phi(S^a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD^a=C\\\\odot\\\\phi'(S^a),\\\\qquad\\nP^a=B^\\\\top D^a,\\n\\\\qquad\\ne_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand write\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle u,v\\\\rangle_n=\\\\frac1n u^\\\\top v,\\n\\\\qquad\\nu\\\\otimes_n v=\\\\frac1nuv^\\\\top.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS^a=BH^a,\\\\qquad\\nf_a=\\\\langle C,G^a\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWith\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\gamma=\\\\frac2p,\\\\qquad\\nR_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe finite-width gradient-flow equations corresponding to the stated mobilities are exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot w_i\\n=\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\gamma}{d}\\n\\\\sum_{a=1}^p\\ne_a\\\\,\\\\phi'(U_i^a)P_i^a\\\\,x_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nequivalently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot U_i^a\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_{b=1}^p\\ne_bR_{ab}\\\\phi'(U_i^b)P_i^b,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot B\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_{a=1}^p e_aD^a\\\\otimes_n H^a,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot C\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_{a=1}^p e_aG^a.\\n\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe three NTK blocks are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(1)}_{ab}\\n=\\nR_{ab}\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\n\\\\phi'(U^a)P^a,\\\\,\\n\\\\phi'(U^b)P^b\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(2)}_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\langle H^a,H^b\\\\rangle_n\\n\\\\langle D^a,D^b\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(3)}_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\langle G^a,G^b\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot f=-\\\\gamma\\\\Theta e,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot L=-\\\\gamma^2 e^\\\\top\\\\Theta e\\\\le0,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Theta=\\\\Theta^{(1)}+\\\\Theta^{(2)}+\\\\Theta^{(3)}.\\n\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll three matrices in (2) are positive semidefinite.\\n\\n## 2. Fixed-mesh width limit\\n\\nIntroduce a smooth saturation \\\\(\\\\tau_R\\\\) satisfying\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\tau_R(x)=x\\\\quad(|x|\\\\le R),\\\\qquad\\n|\\\\tau_R(x)|\\\\le \\\\min\\\\{|x|,R+1\\\\},\\\\qquad\\n|\\\\tau_R'(x)|\\\\le1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand replace \\\\(P^a\\\\) by \\\\(\\\\tau_R(P^a)\\\\) only in the \\\\(w\\\\)-update.\\n\\nFor a fixed mesh \\\\(h\\\\), the learned middle matrix obeys\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_k\\n=\\nB_0-\\\\gamma h\\n\\\\sum_{r<k}\\\\sum_{b=1}^p\\ne_{r,b}D_r^b\\\\otimes_nH_r^b.\\n\\\\tag{4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_k^a\\n=\\nB_0H_k^a\\n-\\\\gamma h\\n\\\\sum_{r<k,b}\\ne_{r,b}D_r^b\\n\\\\langle H_r^b,H_k^a\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\tag{5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_k^a\\n=\\nB_0^\\\\top D_k^a\\n-\\\\gamma h\\n\\\\sum_{r<k,b}\\ne_{r,b}H_r^b\\n\\\\langle D_r^b,D_k^a\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\tag{6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus, for fixed \\\\(h,R,T\\\\), the entire \\\\(N=\\\\lceil T/h\\\\rceil\\\\)-step computation is a finite Tensor Program involving only:\\n\\n- the initial Gaussian matrix \\\\(B_0\\\\) and \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\);\\n- finitely many coordinate maps;\\n- normalized empirical Moment scalars.\\n\\nThe exact theorem used here is the Tensor Program Master Theorem: fixed finite programs with Gaussian initial matrices and vectors, coordinatewise pseudo-Lipschitz maps, adaptive Moment scalars, and reused matrix/transpose products have almost-sure empirical pseudo-Lipschitz limits. Its ZMatMul rule includes the reused-adjoint response term and permits singular Gaussian covariances. These are precisely Setup G.2, Definition G.3, Theorem G.4, and Remark L.2 in the [official Tensor Programs IV supplement](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/yang21c/yang21c-supp.pdf), based on the no-rank-stability theorem proved in [Tensor Programs III](https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10685).\\n\\nWe use this theorem only at fixed finite \\\\(N\\\\); no continuous-time conclusion is imported from it.\\n\\n## 3. The limiting action state\\n\\nEnumerate the countable two-sided grammar generated by\\n\\n\\\\[\\nw^1,\\\\ldots,w^d,\\\\quad C,\\\\quad 1,\\\\quad B_0,\\\\quad B_0^\\\\ast,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nrational linear combinations, the named bounded coordinate maps, and normalized moments. For each finite same-side tuple, the Tensor Program theorem supplies its limiting joint law.\\n\\nThe laws are projectively consistent, so they define right and left probability spaces. Quotient the rational probe spans by the limiting \\\\(L^2\\\\)-null seminorm and complete them to Hilbert spaces\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal H_R,\\\\qquad \\\\mathcal H_L.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe initial Gaussian matrix becomes an actual bounded operator. Indeed, using \\\\(1/4\\\\)-nets,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb P(\\\\|B_0\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}>10)\\n\\\\le\\n2\\\\,9^{2n}e^{-25n/2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhich is summable. Hence, eventually almost surely,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|B_0v\\\\|_n\\\\le10\\\\|v\\\\|_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPassing this inequality to limiting probe Grams gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|B_0v\\\\|_2\\\\le10\\\\|v\\\\|_2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSimilarly, passing\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\langle B_0v,u\\\\rangle_n\\n=\\n\\\\langle v,B_0^\\\\top u\\\\rangle_n\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngives a genuine adjoint pair\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_0:\\\\mathcal H_R\\\\to\\\\mathcal H_L,\\n\\\\qquad\\nB_0^\\\\ast:\\\\mathcal H_L\\\\to\\\\mathcal H_R.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOnly products of an \\\\(L^2\\\\) variable with a bounded coordinate function are used; no false claim that arbitrary \\\\(L^2\\\\) products remain in \\\\(L^2\\\\) is needed.\\n\\nThe limiting current state is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathfrak S=(w,C,B),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nnot \\\\(B_0\\\\) or the training history. Its autonomous equations are\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU^a=w^\\\\top x_a,\\\\qquad\\nH^a=\\\\phi(U^a),\\\\qquad\\nS^a=BH^a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nG^a=\\\\phi(S^a),\\\\qquad\\nD^a=C\\\\phi'(S^a),\\\\qquad\\nP^a=B^\\\\ast D^a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_a=\\\\mathbb E_L[CG^a],\\\\qquad e_a=f_a-y_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\dot w\\n&=\\n-\\\\frac{\\\\gamma}{d}\\n\\\\sum_a e_a\\\\phi'(U^a)P^a x_a,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot B\\n&=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_a e_aD^a\\\\otimes H^a,\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\dot C\\n&=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_a e_aG^a.\\n\\\\end{aligned}}\\n\\\\tag{7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(u\\\\otimes v)q=u\\\\,\\\\mathbb E_R[vq].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis depends only on the current action state, so restartability follows once uniqueness is proved.\\n\\n## 4. Cutoff flow and dimension-free Euler control\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nb=\\\\max\\\\{\\\\|\\\\phi\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\|\\\\phi'\\\\|_\\\\infty,\\\\|\\\\phi''\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\}\\\\le2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf \\\\(c(t)=\\\\|C(t)\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\), then\\n\\n\\\\[\\nc'(t)\\\\le 2b(1+bc(t)),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso\\n\\n\\\\[\\nc(t)\\\\le\\\\frac{e^{2b^2t}-1}{b}.\\n\\\\tag{8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAlso,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|\\\\dot B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\n\\\\le\\n2(1+bc)c\\\\,b^2.\\n\\\\tag{9}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese bounds are independent of width and cutoff.\\n\\nFor fixed \\\\(R\\\\), the cutoff vector field is locally Lipschitz in\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|w-\\\\widetilde w\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\n\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_{L^2}\\n+\\n\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe estimates are direct:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|H\\\\|_2\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|H-\\\\widetilde H\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\nb\\\\|C-\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_2\\n+b\\\\|\\\\widetilde C\\\\|_\\\\infty\\\\|S-\\\\widetilde S\\\\|_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|P-\\\\widetilde P\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\|B-\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|D\\\\|_2\\n+\\\\|\\\\widetilde B\\\\|_{\\\\rm op}\\\\|D-\\\\widetilde D\\\\|_2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe cutoff bounds the only otherwise problematic multiplier in the \\\\(w\\\\)-equation.\\n\\nIf \\\\(F_R\\\\) denotes this vector field, then on the reached region\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|F_R(x)-F_R(y)\\\\|\\\\le L_R\\\\|x-y\\\\|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|F_R(x)\\\\|\\\\le M_R,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith \\\\(L_R,M_R\\\\) independent of \\\\(n\\\\). The elementary Euler recurrence therefore gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{kh\\\\le T}\\n\\\\|\\\\theta_{n,R}(kh)-\\\\theta_{n,R}^{h}(kh)\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac{M_R}{2}(e^{L_RT}-1)h.\\n\\\\tag{10}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe same estimate holds in the limiting action spaces. Combining (10) with the fixed-mesh Tensor Program theorem proves uniform compact-time convergence of every cutoff flow.\\n\\n## 5. All-source response bound\\n\\nThis is the missing uniform-in-number-of-steps estimate.\\n\\nSerialize each step in the order\\n\\n\\\\[\\nH_k\\\\longrightarrow B_0H_k\\n\\\\longrightarrow S_k,D_k\\n\\\\longrightarrow B_0^\\\\ast D_k\\n\\\\longrightarrow P_k.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe Tensor Program ZDot identity gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS_k^a\\n=\\n\\\\widehat S_k^a\\n+\\\\sum_{r<k,b}\\\\alpha_{ka;rb}D_r^b\\n-\\\\gamma h\\\\sum_{r<k,b}e_{r,b}D_r^bq_{rb;ka},\\n\\\\tag{11}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP_k^a\\n=\\n\\\\widehat P_k^a\\n+\\\\sum_{r\\\\le k,b}\\\\beta_{ka;rb}H_r^b\\n-\\\\gamma h\\\\sum_{r<k,b}e_{r,b}H_r^br_{rb;ka},\\n\\\\tag{12}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nq_{rb;ka}=\\\\mathbb E[H_r^bH_k^a],\\n\\\\qquad\\nr_{rb;ka}=\\\\mathbb E[D_r^bD_k^a],\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\alpha_{ka;rb}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_k^a}{\\\\partial\\\\widehat P_r^b},\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\beta_{ka;rb}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial D_k^a}{\\\\partial\\\\widehat S_r^b}.\\n\\\\tag{13}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAll Moment scalars are held fixed under these source derivatives, exactly as required by the ZDot definition.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA=\\n\\\\sup_{k,a}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\n\\\\sum_{r<k,b}\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial H_k^a}{\\\\partial\\\\widehat P_r^b}\\n\\\\right|,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB=\\n\\\\sup_{k,a}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\n\\\\sum_{r\\\\le k,b}\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial D_k^a}{\\\\partial\\\\widehat S_r^b}\\n\\\\right|,\\n\\\\qquad\\nK=\\\\sup_{k,a}\\\\|P_k^a\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|X\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}\\n=\\n\\\\inf\\\\left\\\\{s>0:\\\\mathbb E e^{X^2/s^2}\\\\le2\\\\right\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOn \\\\(C_\\\\infty\\\\le1\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\gamma\\\\sum_a|e_a|\\n\\\\le\\n2(1+b)=:\\\\Lambda=6.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathfrak a=\\\\Lambda b^2T=24T,\\\\qquad\\nc=\\\\Lambda bT,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\delta=2\\\\mathfrak a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mu=\\\\Lambda(cb)^2T\\\\le\\\\mathfrak a^3.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor forward sources, write \\\\(s\\\\) and \\\\(d\\\\) for the maximum total source derivatives of \\\\(S\\\\) and \\\\(D\\\\). Differentiating (11), the response terms and learned rank terms give\\n\\n\\\\[\\ns\\\\le1+(A+\\\\mathfrak a)d.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe \\\\(C\\\\)-derivative contributes at most \\\\(\\\\mathfrak a s\\\\), and differentiating \\\\(C\\\\phi'(S)\\\\) contributes another \\\\(\\\\mathfrak a s\\\\). Hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd\\\\le\\\\delta s.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB\\\\le\\n\\\\frac{\\\\delta}{1-\\\\delta(A+\\\\mathfrak a)}.\\n\\\\tag{14}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor adjoint sources, let \\\\(u\\\\) be the total derivative of \\\\(U\\\\). Since \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le b\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{total derivative of }H\\\\le bu.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFrom (12),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{total derivative of }P\\n\\\\le\\n1+(B+\\\\mu)bu.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDifferentiating the \\\\(U\\\\)-update gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nu_{k+1}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\left[\\n1+\\\\Lambda bhZ_k\\n+\\\\Lambda b^2h(B+\\\\mu)\\n\\\\right]u_k\\n+\\\\Lambda bh,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nZ_k=\\\\max_{a\\\\le p}|P_k^a|.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDiscrete Grönwall yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\nbu\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\mathfrak a\\n\\\\exp\\\\left\\\\{\\n\\\\Lambda bh\\\\sum_kZ_k\\n+\\\\mathfrak a(B+\\\\mu)\\n\\\\right\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTo average the first exponential, generalized Hölder across the \\\\(N\\\\) time indices gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E e^{\\\\Lambda bh\\\\sum_kZ_k}\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\prod_k\\n\\\\left(\\n\\\\mathbb E e^{\\\\Lambda bT Z_k}\\n\\\\right)^{1/N}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E e^{s|X|}\\n\\\\le\\n2e^{s^2K^2/4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhenever \\\\(\\\\|X\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}\\\\le K\\\\), a union bound over the \\\\(p\\\\) samples gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA\\n\\\\le\\n2p\\\\mathfrak a\\n\\\\exp\\\\left\\\\{\\n\\\\mathfrak a(B+\\\\mu)\\n+\\\\frac{(\\\\Lambda bTK)^2}{4}\\n\\\\right\\\\}.\\n\\\\tag{15}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFinally, \\\\(\\\\widehat P_k^a\\\\) is Gaussian with standard deviation at most \\\\(cb=\\\\mathfrak a\\\\), while the two shifts in (12) are bounded by \\\\(bB\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\mu\\\\). Therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK\\\\le4\\\\mathfrak a+4b(B+\\\\mu).\\n\\\\tag{16}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathfrak a\\\\le\\\\frac1{64p},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthen the bootstrap hypothesis \\\\(A\\\\le1/4\\\\) gives, using (14)–(16),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB\\\\le4\\\\mathfrak a,\\\\qquad\\nK\\\\le40\\\\mathfrak a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the right-hand side of (15) is strictly smaller than \\\\(1/4\\\\). A first-crossing argument therefore proves\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\nA\\\\le\\\\frac14,\\\\qquad\\nB\\\\le4\\\\mathfrak a,\\\\qquad\\nK\\\\le40\\\\mathfrak a\\n}\\n\\\\tag{17}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nuniformly in \\\\(R\\\\), \\\\(h\\\\), and \\\\(N=T/h\\\\). This controls every past source; no response hierarchy is truncated.\\n\\nSince \\\\(\\\\mathfrak a=24T\\\\), condition (17) holds on\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT\\\\le\\\\frac1{1536p}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n## 6. Removing the cutoff\\n\\nLet \\\\(X_n\\\\) and \\\\(X_{n,R}\\\\) be the uncut and cutoff finite-width flows. The exact identity\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\phi'(U)P-\\\\phi'(U_R)\\\\tau_R(P_R)\\n={}&\\n\\\\phi'(U)(P-P_R)\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\phi'(U)\\\\bigl(P_R-\\\\tau_R(P_R)\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\\\n&+\\\\bigl(\\\\phi'(U)-\\\\phi'(U_R)\\\\bigr)\\\\tau_R(P_R)\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]\\n\\ngives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nE_{n,R}'(t)\\n\\\\le\\nC_T(1+R)E_{n,R}(t)\\n+\\nC_T\\\\sum_a\\n\\\\|P_{n,R}^a-\\\\tau_R(P_{n,R}^a)\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\tag{18}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(E_{n,R}\\\\) is the mixed \\\\(L^2\\\\)/operator-norm state distance.\\n\\nFrom (17),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|P_R^a-\\\\tau_R(P_R^a)\\\\|_2\\n\\\\le\\nC(K+R)e^{-R^2/(2K^2)}.\\n\\\\tag{19}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor fixed \\\\(R\\\\), the squared tail map is pseudo-Lipschitz, so fixed-cutoff convergence passes (19) to the finite-width \\\\(\\\\limsup\\\\). Thus (18) yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\limsup_{n\\\\to\\\\infty}\\n\\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}E_{n,R}(t)\\n\\\\le\\nC_Te^{C_TR}(K+R)e^{-R^2/(2K^2)}\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\tag{20}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe Gaussian \\\\(R^2\\\\)-decay dominates the linear Grönwall exponent. Therefore the actual uncut finite-width flow converges to the cutoff-independent limiting flow.\\n\\nThe same comparison proves uniqueness: compare any \\\\(L^2\\\\)-strong solution with the canonical cutoff solution. The only tail in the estimate belongs to the canonical cutoff solution, so no tail assumption is needed on the competitor. Letting \\\\(R\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) makes their distance zero.\\n\\nConsequently (7) is autonomous, unique, and restartable.\\n\\n## 7. Path laws and exact GD\\n\\nFor either hidden layer and every time interval \\\\(I\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i\\n\\\\sup_{s,t\\\\in I}|z_i(s)-z_i(t)|^2\\n\\\\le\\n|I|\\\\int_I\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_i|\\\\dot z_i(u)|^2\\\\,du.\\n\\\\tag{21}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor a fixed finite time grid, Tensor Programs gives joint \\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence at all grid points; fourth-moment tests give the needed uniform integrability. Equation (21) makes the path law \\\\(W_2\\\\)-close to its grid interpolation uniformly in \\\\(n\\\\). Sending the mesh to zero proves\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\overline\\\\mu_n^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\overline\\\\mu^{(\\\\ell)}\\n\\\\quad\\\\text{in }W_2(C([0,T])),\\n\\\\qquad \\\\ell=1,2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe same argument passes the integrated velocity energies.\\n\\nIt remains to compare exact GD with the finite-width ODE. On the stopped region determined by (8)–(9),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|DF_n\\\\|\\\\le C_Tn^4,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|DF_nF_n\\\\|\\\\le C_Tn^8.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese deliberately loose bounds follow by direct coordinate counting: each derivative contains at most two width sums, while all derivatives of \\\\(\\\\sin+\\\\cos\\\\) are bounded.\\n\\nThe stopped Euler estimate is therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{k\\\\eta_{0,n}\\\\le T}\\n\\\\|\\\\theta_{n,k}-\\\\theta_n(k\\\\eta_{0,n})\\\\|\\n\\\\le\\nC_T\\\\eta_{0,n}n^8e^{C_Tn^4T}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\eta_{0,n}=e^{-n^{10}},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthis tends to zero. The continuous flow stays strictly inside enlarged stopping bounds, so the stopped estimate closes by the usual first-exit contradiction. Hence this is a genuinely joint width/GD limit.\\n\\n## 8. Strict nonlinearity, activity, and kernel motion\\n\\nAt initialization,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU=(U^1,\\\\ldots,U^p)\\\\sim N(0,R).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{ab}=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(U^a)\\\\phi(U^b)].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(\\\\phi=\\\\sin+\\\\cos\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{ab}=e^{-1+R_{ab}}\\n=\\n\\\\exp\\\\left(-\\\\frac{\\\\|x_a-x_b\\\\|^2}{2d}\\\\right).\\n\\\\tag{22}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor distinct inputs, \\\\(Q\\\\succ0\\\\): by the Gaussian Fourier representation,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nv^\\\\top Qv\\n=\\nc_d\\\\int_{\\\\mathbb R^d}\\ne^{-d\\\\|\\\\omega\\\\|^2/2}\\n\\\\left|\\\\sum_a v_ae^{i\\\\omega^\\\\top x_a}\\\\right|^2d\\\\omega,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhich is zero only when \\\\(v=0\\\\).\\n\\nThe initial second preactivation satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\nS\\\\sim N(0,Q),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nindependently of \\\\(U\\\\). Its postactivation kernel is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nJ_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi(S^a)\\\\phi(S^b)]\\n=\\ne^{-1+Q_{ab}}.\\n\\\\tag{23}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSince\\n\\n\\\\[\\nJ=e^{-1}\\\\sum_{k=0}^\\\\infty\\\\frac{Q^{\\\\circ k}}{k!},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe Schur product theorem and the strictly positive \\\\(k=1\\\\) term give \\\\(J\\\\succ0\\\\).\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\ng(S)=\\\\sum_a y_a\\\\phi(S^a),\\n\\\\qquad\\nV_a=y_ag(S)\\\\phi'(S^a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM_{ab}=\\\\mathbb E[V_aV_b],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Gamma_{ba}\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\partial V_a}{\\\\partial S^b}\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet \\\\(Y\\\\sim N(0,M)\\\\), independent of \\\\(U\\\\), and set\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Xi_a\\n=\\nY_a+\\\\sum_b\\\\Gamma_{ba}\\\\phi(U^b).\\n\\\\tag{24}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquation (24) is exactly the reused-adjoint law for \\\\(B_0^\\\\ast V\\\\).\\n\\nDefine the two activation/data Gaussian integrals\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_2\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{a,b}Q_{ab}M_{ab},\\n\\\\tag{25}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_1\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{a,b}\\nR_{ab}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\n\\\\phi'(U^a)\\\\phi'(U^b)\\\\Xi_a\\\\Xi_b\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\tag{26}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth are strictly positive.\\n\\nIndeed, if \\\\(q^\\\\top Mq=0\\\\), then the full-support Gaussian law of \\\\(S\\\\) and analyticity imply\\n\\n\\\\[\\ng(s)\\\\sum_aq_ay_a\\\\phi'(s_a)\\\\equiv0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first factor is not identically zero. Hence the second factor vanishes identically. Differentiating with respect to \\\\(s_a\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nq_ay_a\\\\phi''(s_a)\\\\equiv0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso \\\\(q=0\\\\). Thus \\\\(M\\\\succ0\\\\).\\n\\nTherefore \\\\(Q\\\\circ M\\\\succ0\\\\), proving \\\\(A_2>0\\\\). Also, conditioning (26) on \\\\(U\\\\) and keeping only the independent Gaussian part \\\\(Y\\\\) gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_1\\n\\\\ge\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\nz(U)^\\\\top(R\\\\circ M)z(U)\\n\\\\right]>0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nz_a(U)=\\\\phi'(U^a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nbecause \\\\(R\\\\circ M\\\\succ0\\\\) whenever \\\\(M\\\\succ0\\\\), \\\\(R\\\\succeq0\\\\), and \\\\(R_{aa}=1\\\\).\\n\\nDirectly differentiating (7) at zero gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\nC'(0)=\\\\gamma g,\\n\\\\qquad\\nD_a'(0)=\\\\gamma y_aV_a,\\n\\\\qquad\\nP_a'(0)=\\\\gamma y_a\\\\Xi_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB'(0)=0,\\\\qquad\\nw'(0)=0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nU_a''(0)\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2\\n\\\\sum_bR_{ab}\\\\phi'(U^b)\\\\Xi_b.\\n\\\\tag{27}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUsing \\\\(S=BH\\\\), the adjoint identity, and (24) gives the exact NTK expansions\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(1)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2A_1t^2+O(t^3),\\n\\\\tag{28}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(2)}(t)y\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2A_2t^2+O(t^3),\\n\\\\tag{29}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta^{(3)}(t)y\\n=\\ny^\\\\top Jy\\n+\\\\gamma^2(A_1+A_2)t^2\\n+O(t^3).\\n\\\\tag{30}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\ny^\\\\top\\\\Theta(t)y\\n=\\ny^\\\\top Jy\\n+\\n2\\\\gamma^2(A_1+A_2)t^2\\n+O(t^3).\\n}\\n\\\\tag{31}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe remainders in (28)–(31) are genuine: rerunning (17) on \\\\([0,t]\\\\) gives \\\\(\\\\|P(s)\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}\\\\le C t\\\\), which justifies two differentiations of the integral equations by dominated convergence and bounds the third-order integral remainder.\\n\\nEquation (31) proves that the full NTK matrix is nonconstant. The scalar label-direction kernel\\n\\n\\\\[\\nK_{\\\\mathrm{eff}}(t)=\\\\frac1p y^\\\\top\\\\Theta(t)y\\n\\\\]\\n\\nalso moves and reduces to the ordinary scalar kernel when \\\\(p=1\\\\).\\n\\nSince each block is positive semidefinite, (28)–(29) also imply positive trace activity for the first two blocks; the third block is already strictly positive at initialization.\\n\\nFrom (27),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac1p\\\\sum_a\\n\\\\mathbb E|\\\\dot U^a(t)|^2\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\gamma^4t^2}{p}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\|RZ\\\\|^2+O(t^3),\\n\\\\qquad\\nZ_a=\\\\phi'(U^a)\\\\Xi_a,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the leading coefficient is positive. Similarly,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_a\\\\mathbb E[V_aS_a''(0)]\\n=\\n\\\\gamma^2(A_1+A_2)>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso \\\\(S''(0)\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\). Thus both hidden layers have positive integrated speed and nonzero \\\\(L^2\\\\) displacement for every sufficiently small positive time.\\n\\nFinally, if \\\\(Z\\\\sim N(0,1)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi(Z)^2=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi(Z)=e^{-1/2},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathbb E[Z\\\\phi(Z)]=e^{-1/2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\inf_{a,b}\\n\\\\mathbb E|\\\\phi(Z)-aZ-b|^2\\n=\\n1-\\\\frac2e>0.\\n\\\\tag{32}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth hidden marginals equal \\\\(N(0,1)\\\\) at initialization. Path-\\\\(W_2\\\\) continuity, positive initial variance, and (32) preserve positive variance and nonlinear residual on a sufficiently short interval.\\n\\nMoreover,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf(0)=0,\\\\qquad L(0)=1,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand by (3),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL'(0)\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma^2y^\\\\top Jy<0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus \\\\(L(T)<L(0)\\\\) for every sufficiently small \\\\(T>0\\\\).\\n\\n## Audit verdict\\n\\nThe result survives independent audits of:\\n\\n- the exact scaling and finite-width equations;\\n- the primary Tensor Program theorem and all its hypotheses;\\n- reused \\\\(B_0/B_0^\\\\top\\\\) responses, including singular covariances;\\n- the all-source \\\\(A\\\\)-\\\\(B\\\\)-\\\\(K\\\\) bootstrap;\\n- the action-state/GNS construction;\\n- finite-width and limiting cutoff removal;\\n- path-\\\\(W_2\\\\) convergence;\\n- the exact-GD diagonal estimate;\\n- all initialization coefficients and positivity arguments;\\n- arbitrary label signs, singular input Grams, antipodal inputs, and near-duplicate inputs.\\n\\nThe necessary qualifications are only:\\n\\n1. \\\\(p,d\\\\) and the dataset are fixed independently of \\\\(n\\\\).\\n2. No activity lower bound is uniform over all distinct datasets.\\n3. Kernel movement means movement of the full NTK matrix—or equivalently here its label-direction quadratic form—not an unproved trace-motion statement.\\n\\nWith those mathematically necessary clarifications, the theorem is unconditional.\\n\\nGoal work used 148,600 tokens over about 33 minutes.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a044b8-689f-7231-8d7c-b466f17b520d\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787859331,\"completedAt\":1787860608,\"durationMs\":1277344,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1394\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"can you check and fully audit the proof of the theorem you have proven above ? run several diverse and independent and adversarial audits to check all aspects of the proof, from step by step arguments, invocations of theorems (obligations of theorems, assertions, do they match with the invocation?), and all other other aspects, to see if the theorem proof is rigorous and valid, fix any issues you find and issue your corrects, if you did have any corrections you must run the audits rounds again to catch if they now pass new independent audits, do this enough times until the audits pass all clean and completely&#x20;\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1395\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning audit with math solving**\",\"**Preparing multiple isolated audit agents**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1396\",\"text\":\"I’m using the rigorous-math skill for this audit. I’ll treat the displayed theorem as untrusted: one audit will check the finite-width scaling and dynamics, one will check the Han/GFOM invocation against the full primary source, and one will attack the response/GNS/cutoff bridge. I will then reconcile their findings, repair any issue, and rerun fresh adversarial checks on the repaired statement. I will not read project files.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1397\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning multiple agent spawning**\",\"**Planning isolated comprehensive context**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_55DZfZaG7fOkGkli68J6sLWb\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044b8-bc2c-7bf3-b4cb-eed30cf879f9\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_dynamics_activity\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_tudBxwrwwZ1KS0SJEBvY7Idx\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044b8-d53b-7553-a008-dc6d117a59b4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_han_invocation\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_rMcZzqFeGoNzBr8D6HuX4XzK\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044b8-ee23-7f00-8e58-95f150acd373\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_response_operator\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1398\",\"summary\":[\"**Inspecting finite dynamics scaling**\",\"**Verifying kernel and gradient calculations**\",\"**Confirming gradient and kernel movement formulas**\",\"**Verifying kernel positivity and covariance structure**\",\"**Verifying covariance convergence and theorem assumptions**\",\"**Analyzing state evolution with coordinate-specific marks**\",\"**Assessing GNS state autonomy and convergence**\",\"**Verifying derivative inequality details**\",\"**Bounding weighted response terms**\",\"**Verifying response tail continuity**\",\"**Verifying L2 boundedness and cutoff flow**\",\"**Confirming uniqueness and bounding polynomial growth**\",\"**Deriving crude bound exponent**\",\"**Estimating Jacobian row sums**\",\"**Verifying uniform integrability requirements**\",\"**Clarifying theorem conditions and positivity properties**\",\"**Verifying theorem source and GNS details**\",\"**Analyzing mean-field evolution and joint state construction**\",\"**Confirming cutoff uniqueness and operator norm continuity**\",\"**Verifying convergence of empirical distributions**\",\"**Planning source retrieval from temp file**\",\"**Verifying matrix symmetry assumption**\",\"**Clarifying theorem assumptions and conditions**\",\"**Mapping forward and backward stage definitions**\",\"**Clarifying recursive state and error definitions**\",\"**Assessing empirical averages and saturation effects**\",\"**Planning status queries**\",\"**Auditing response constants and error bounds**\",\"**Verifying coefficient bounds and derivative estimates**\",\"**Verifying theta and bound calculations**\",\"**Analyzing coordinate-dependent response coefficients**\",\"**Deriving explicit canonical state formula**\",\"**Formulating lemma with cavity formula**\",\"**Establishing induction bounds on scalar differences**\",\"**Formulating joint convergence via dyadic mesh**\",\"**Assessing feasibility of full proof**\",\"**Verifying NTK coefficient positivity**\",\"**Confirming positivity of trace lower bound**\",\"**Planning lemma correction and audit follow-up**\",\"**Acquiring final Han value**\",\"**Planning lemma correction and audit**\",\"**Formulating detailed theorem corrections**\",\"**Acknowledging audit commentary gaps**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1399\",\"text\":\"The first audit round did not pass cleanly. The finite-width algebra and dataset-separation argument survived, but three substantive problems were found:\\n\\n- the proof treated the adaptive Euler scheme as if Han’s theorem applied directly, although only a deterministic-scalar oracle is row-separable;\\n- the action-law/GNS and continuous-time lifting were compressed into assertions not supplied by Han;\\n- the local feature-learning calculation omitted the exact reused-adjoint coefficient and left the multi-sample meanings of \\\\(K^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\), \\\\(V^{(\\\\ell)}\\\\), and the nonlinear residual ambiguous.\\n\\nI am correcting the theorem rather than defending those omissions. The next audit round will test explicit oracle-coupling, side-mark convergence, action-state construction, cutoff transfer, and corrected local coefficients.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1400\",\"summary\":[\"**Defining deterministic oracle coefficients**\",\"**Formulating recursive coefficient updates**\",\"**Verifying convergence bounds and induction steps**\",\"**Defining bounded probe grammar and empirical coupling**\",\"**Extending joint law representation via Kolmogorov consistency**\",\"**Proving uniform Euler scheme convergence and response bounds**\",\"**Analyzing cutoff removal and action convergence**\",\"**Bounding polynomial derivatives for audit**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_0GWLXbr6h1gbB41uOJTxpR8l\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044b8-d53b-7553-a008-dc6d117a59b4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_han_invocation\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_QJxxQOp5LgzbhylUTJK4ZDfA\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044b8-ee23-7f00-8e58-95f150acd373\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/audit_response_operator\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_5mMM1njXdcLlWsnHazZBkkmE\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0