\"summary\":[\"**Resolving DOI link with curl**\",\"**Inspecting HTML for redirect**\",\"**Inspecting Project Euclid PDF download URLs**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-606719a7-f8b9-4653-bd10-a81b3586942e\",\"query\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-53/issue-4/Entrywise-dynamics-and-universality-of-general-first-order-methods/10.1214/25-AOS2544.full.pdf\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-53/issue-4/Entrywise-dynamics-and-universality-of-general-first-order-methods/10.1214/25-AOS2544.full.pdf\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-866f70b0-b786-496c-9c12-786805efedec\",\"query\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf/10.1214/25-AOS2544\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf/10.1214/25-AOS2544\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-591d92e1-b78c-4b5a-bb65-65e60b2dc454\",\"query\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-53/issue-4/Entrywise-dynamics-and-universality-of-general-first-order-methods/10.1214/25-AOS2544.pdf\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-53/issue-4/Entrywise-dynamics-and-universality-of-general-first-order-methods/10.1214/25-AOS2544.pdf\"}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-42af0eb1-2bd8-4e01-8299-13ee39634800\",\"query\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.aos/10.1214/25-AOS2544\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.aos/10.1214/25-AOS2544\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1412\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning parallel curl execution**\",\"**Planning DOI metadata retrieval**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-5ffe7580-d847-4ba6-a32b-2d5e2b41f1c4\",\"query\":\"https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/25-AOS2544\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/25-AOS2544\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1413\",\"summary\":[\"**Inspecting project links**\",\"**Exploring Crossref relation queries**\",\"**Downloading content from URL**\",\"**Exploring proxy options for fetching supplement**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-8a89045d-567a-43aa-9ea8-ff097498c0b0\",\"query\":\"https://r.jina.ai/http://projecteuclid.org/journals/supplementalcontent/10.1214/25-AOS2544/aos2544supp.pdf\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://r.jina.ai/http://projecteuclid.org/journals/supplementalcontent/10.1214/25-AOS2544/aos2544supp.pdf\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1414\",\"summary\":[\"**Inspecting theorem proof start**\",\"**Noticing restricted PDF access**\",\"**Evaluating proof supplement retrieval options**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-f82ddf64-c022-4669-882c-82d653d64deb\",\"query\":\"\\\"aos2544supp.pdf\\\" ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"\\\"aos2544supp.pdf\\\"\",\"\\\"Supplement: Proofs\\\" \\\"Entrywise dynamics\\\" Han\",\"\\\"25-AOS2544SUPP\\\" PDF\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1415\",\"summary\":[\"**Downloading arxiv v1 source for comparison**\",\"**Extracting version 1**\",\"**Inspecting asymmetric SE proof subsection**\",\"**Comparing theorem assumptions and proofs**\",\"**Planning theorem audit and corrections**\",\"**Formulating corrected finite-step theorem**\",\"**Planning formula incorporation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a044c2-4150-7272-ad27-4c2b2836427c\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044c2-3e2a-7441-bca5-22cdfb18ddae\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/round3_foundations\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a044c2-2fa0-7af1-9faf-7003aa47ccc3\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044c2-2c4d-72c2-9f83-da580d2031a5\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/round3_constructive\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1416\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning final theorem correction and audit**\",\"**Outlining final audit report with corrections**\",\"**Detailing corrected theorem architecture and parameters**\",\"**Verifying initial output kernel equality**\",\"**Confirming SPD property of kernel T**\",\"**Clarifying local expansions and kernel definitions**\",\"**Outlining finite-step operator theorem limitations**\",\"**Clarifying theorem proof availability**\",\"**Summarizing audit failure on main theorem**\",\"**Planning formal theorem retraction**\",\"**Analyzing scalar ledger formulation**\",\"**Clarifying theorem source and citation scope**\",\"**Auditing loss derivative discrepancies**\",\"**Confirming output layer activation dynamics**\",\"**Planning final meta-audit of conclusion**\",\"**Initiating final independent audit**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1417\",\"text\":\"Three independent rounds now agree on the same boundary: the algebra and local nonlinear feature-learning mechanism survive, but the compact-time joint limit does not. I’ve corrected one additional hidden flaw—\\\\(P\\\\in L^2\\\\) is not enough for cutoff uniqueness; the admissible solution class would need a restart-stable sub-Gaussian response bound—and I’m doing one final audit of the narrowed, corrected claims before issuing the verdict.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_o3wb4W5XqXaHNSXROH4DBx2o\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044c8-0c8e-7d21-b37d-03270dbdbde4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/final_corrected_claims\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1418\",\"summary\":[\"**Confirming citation sources and validity**\",\"**Clarifying theorem proof availability and status**\",\"**Distinguishing proven results from open propositions**\",\"**Clarifying lemma conditions and quantifiers**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a044c8-0fdb-7ae2-ae74-c78eeefacbc1\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044c8-0c8e-7d21-b37d-03270dbdbde4\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/final_corrected_claims\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1419\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning concise final answer with citations**\",\"**Listing audit blockers and certified portions**\",\"**Including final audit formulas**\",\"**Formulating exact NTK dynamics and identities**\",\"**Detailing kernel moment computations and proof status**\",\"**Verifying exact loss derivative at initialization**\",\"**Planning hyperparameter audit summary table**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1420\",\"text\":\"No—the previously claimed compact-time joint mean-field/gradient-flow theorem does not pass a rigorous audit. I withdraw the claim that it was proved. Three independent audit rounds, followed by a final audit of the narrowed corrections, reached the same conclusion.\\n\\nNo project files were read during this audit.\\n\\n## Audit verdict\\n\\n| Component | Verdict |\\n|---|---|\\n| Finite-width normalization and gradients | Pass |\\n| Layerwise NTK formulas and scaling | Pass |\\n| Initialization Gaussian geometry | Pass |\\n| Initialization jets and positivity coefficients | Pass after correction |\\n| Han theorem applied to a fixed deterministic oracle | Pass at statement level |\\n| Actual adaptive GD identified with that oracle | Fail—missing proof |\\n| Autonomous action/GNS state | Fail—missing construction |\\n| Fixed-mesh to continuous-time limit | Fail—missing uniform estimates |\\n| Cutoff removal and uniqueness | Fail as stated |\\n| Exact-GD diagonal limit | Fail—derivative/stopping estimates asserted |\\n| Path-Wasserstein and uniform-time convergence | Fail—tightness/UI estimates missing |\\n| Full affirmative theorem | Not proved |\\n\\nNo counterexample to the proposed construction was found. The problem is incompleteness of the proof, not a demonstrated falsehood of the theorem.\\n\\n## What is rigorously correct\\n\\nFor\\n\\n\\\\[\\nL_n=\\\\frac1p\\\\sum_{a=1}^p(f_{n,a}-y_a)^2,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\gamma=\\\\frac2p,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the proposed scaling\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(W^{(1)})=\\\\frac1d,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(B)=\\\\frac1n,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Var}(c)=n^{-4},\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(1)}=\\\\frac nd,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(2)}=1,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\lambda_n^{(3)}=\\\\frac1n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe normalized finite-width equations are exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot U_i^a\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b e_bR_{ab}\\\\phi'(U_i^b)P_i^b,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot B\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b e_bD^b\\\\otimes_n H^b,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\dot C\\n=\\n-\\\\gamma\\\\sum_b e_bG^b,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR_{ab}=\\\\frac{x_a^\\\\top x_b}{d},\\\\quad\\nH^a=\\\\phi(U^a),\\\\quad\\nD^a=C\\\\odot\\\\phi'(S^a),\\\\quad\\nP^a=B^\\\\top D^a.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe NTK blocks are exactly\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(1)}_{ab}\\n=\\nR_{ab}\\n\\\\left\\\\langle\\n\\\\phi'(U^a)P^a,\\\\phi'(U^b)P^b\\n\\\\right\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Theta^{(2)}_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\langle H^a,H^b\\\\rangle_n\\n\\\\langle D^a,D^b\\\\rangle_n,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Theta^{(3)}_{ab}\\n=\\n\\\\langle G^a,G^b\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(\\\\phi(z)=\\\\sin z+\\\\cos z\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{ab}=e^{-1+R_{ab}},\\n\\\\qquad\\nT_{ab}=e^{-1+Q_{ab}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor every fixed collection of pairwise-distinct RMS-normalized inputs, both \\\\(Q\\\\) and \\\\(T\\\\) are strictly positive definite. The initialization nonlinear-regression residual in either hidden marginal is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\inf_{a,b}\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\lvert \\\\phi(Z)-aZ-b\\\\rvert^2\\n=\\n1-\\\\frac2e>0,\\n\\\\qquad Z\\\\sim N(0,1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese constants are dataset-specific; no lower bound is uniform as two distinct inputs approach one another.\\n\\n### Correct interpretation of the local calculation\\n\\nThe earlier formulas must be stated as initialization-jet limits, not as Taylor expansions of an already-established width-first limiting path.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal J_2X_n=\\\\frac12X_n''(0).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSet, for \\\\(S\\\\sim N(0,Q)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\ng(S)=\\\\sum_a y_a\\\\phi(S_a),\\n\\\\qquad\\nV_a(S)=y_ag(S)\\\\phi'(S_a),\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nM=\\\\mathbb E[V(S)V(S)^\\\\top],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Gamma=\\\\mathbb E[DV(S)].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor \\\\(U\\\\sim N(0,R)\\\\), write \\\\(H=\\\\phi(U)\\\\), let\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\xi\\\\sim N(0,M),\\\\qquad \\\\xi\\\\perp U,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT_*=\\\\xi+\\\\Gamma H,\\n\\\\qquad\\nD_U=\\\\operatorname{diag}(\\\\phi'(U)).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_w\\n=\\n\\\\mathbb E\\\\!\\\\left[\\nT_*^\\\\top D_URD_UT_*\\n\\\\right]>0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\nA_B\\n=\\n\\\\mathbf 1^\\\\top(Q\\\\circ M)\\\\mathbf1>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe finite-width initialization jets satisfy\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal J_2\\\\!\\\\left(y^\\\\top\\\\Theta_n^{(1)}y\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\gamma^2A_w,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal J_2\\\\!\\\\left(y^\\\\top\\\\Theta_n^{(2)}y\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\gamma^2A_B,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal J_2\\\\!\\\\left(y^\\\\top\\\\Theta_n^{(3)}y\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n\\\\gamma^2(A_w+A_B),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal J_2\\\\!\\\\left(y^\\\\top\\\\Theta_ny\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow\\n2\\\\gamma^2(A_w+A_B)>0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLikewise,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\dot L_n(0)\\\\longrightarrow\\n-\\\\gamma^2y^\\\\top Ty<0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThese are valid initialization statements. They do not establish a width-first limiting path or justify exchanging \\\\(n\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) with \\\\(t\\\\downarrow0\\\\).\\n\\n## The first fatal gap\\n\\nThe adaptive Euler dynamics are not directly a row-separate deterministic GFOM. After eliminating the learned rank-one part of \\\\(B_k\\\\), the recursion depends on adaptive empirical scalars\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne_{ka},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\langle H_{\\\\ell b},H_{ka}\\\\rangle_n,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\langle D_{\\\\ell b},D_{ka}\\\\rangle_n.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nA valid application of state evolution first requires a deterministic conditional oracle replacing every one of these scalars by its state-evolution value, followed by a same-\\\\(B_0\\\\) coupling proof of the form\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max_{q\\\\le pN}\\nd_n(X_q,\\\\bar X_q)\\n\\\\le\\nC_{h,R,N}\\n\\\\max_{q\\\\le pN}\\\\zeta_q\\n\\\\;\\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathbb P}\\\\;0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe previous proof displayed a schematic inequality of this kind but never:\\n\\n- defined the entire oracle recursively;\\n- serialized every \\\\(B_0\\\\) and \\\\(B_0^\\\\top\\\\) query;\\n- enumerated all empirical scalars;\\n- verified every oracle feed map’s regularity;\\n- or proved the induction for the actual adaptive dynamics.\\n\\nConsequently, the proof stops before the claimed state evolution has been identified.\\n\\n## External-theorem audit\\n\\nHan’s asymmetric GFOM result applies to a fixed finite deterministic history with independent centered sub-Gaussian matrix entries, proportional dimensions, and deterministic row-separate regular maps. It yields fixed-history control of empirical pseudo-Lipschitz observables. It does not supply adaptive-oracle coupling, countably many probes, an operator action state, continuous time, cutoff removal, path convergence, or autonomy. See the [arXiv manuscript](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19061) and the [published article](https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-statistics/volume-53/issue-4/Entrywise-dynamics-and-universality-of-general-first-order-methods/10.1214/25-AOS2544.full).\\n\\nI also checked proof availability. The arXiv manuscript contains an asymmetric-AMP reduction, but that reduction itself invokes an asserted AMP comparison; I did not locate a separate proof of the averaged asymmetric theorem used here. The official proofs are assigned to a [separate supplement](https://projecteuclid.org/journals/supplementalcontent/10.1214/25-AOS2544/aos2544supp.pdf), which was access-restricted. Thus the theorem’s statement and hypotheses were verified, but its complete underlying proof was not independently auditable from the accessible primary text.\\n\\n## Additional necessary corrections\\n\\n- The previous argument proves a positive jet in \\\\(y^\\\\top\\\\Theta_ny\\\\); it does not prove that the scalar trace \\\\(p^{-1}\\\\operatorname{tr}\\\\Theta(t)\\\\) is nonconstant. “Kernel moves” must refer to the full NTK matrix unless a separate trace calculation is supplied.\\n- Uniqueness cannot be claimed merely for \\\\(P\\\\in L^2\\\\). The cutoff comparison contains an \\\\(e^{CRT}\\\\) amplification, which an arbitrary \\\\(L^2\\\\) tail need not overcome. A plausible admissible class is a restart-stable class with a deterministic bound\\n  \\\\[\\n  \\\\sup_{s\\\\le T}\\\\|P(s)\\\\|_{\\\\psi_2}<\\\\infty,\\n  \\\\]\\n  but its invariance and uniqueness still require proof.\\n- The asserted polynomial bounds for the exact-GD vector field and its derivatives were not derived. Choosing a super-exponentially small step does not replace that stopped-region calculation.\\n- Finite-time state evolution does not imply \\\\(W_2(C([0,T]))\\\\) convergence. Uniform \\\\(q>1\\\\) bounds on integrated feature speeds, rational-time identification, equicontinuity, and path-supremum uniform integrability remain necessary.\\n\\nTherefore the corrected, fully audited conclusion is:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{The finite-width algebra, initialization geometry, and initialization jets are valid.}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\\\text{The claimed compact-time joint mean-field/gradient-flow theorem remains unproved.}}\\n\\\\]\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a044c2-5057-79e3-a5b0-e1a646ae6ed5\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a044c2-4d0a-7980-8531-14b9fcc07628\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/round3_external_local\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a042f3-15bb-7ef2-bf9a-b020a8e5fdf5\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787829622,\"completedAt\":1787840538,\"durationMs\":10915891,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1207\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"This is actually a great illuminating example showing the task defined above was not well specified, and a perfectly identical input and perfectly different / opposite label demonstrates that no deterministic learning algorithm can learn any arbitrary configuration that admits this design. So let's add this higher constraint that the samples cannot be \\\"identica with different labels\\\", an even stronger assumption is that no pair of samples can be like that arrangement. However, it is conceivable that the inputs can be made to be arbitrarily close to that configuration but at a constant (albeit small arbitrarily) angle. With this additional constraint, what is the full and complete resolution of the statement? can you prove constructively the existence of one such a model with a joint limit, or can you , similar to proof above, show that such a construction is impossible.\\n\\nnote that full resolution means that you either affirm this by showing a particular architecture and scaling that for ANY configuration of input that does not have duplicate inputs, will have a joint mean field continuous flow limit, or that you prove that NO SUCH SOLUTION EIXITS. anything in between means you have not resolved it. you have to work through this until it is fully and completely resolved. Take full advantage of diversified isolated agents with diversified strategies that look at this from multiple angles (both affirmative and negative resolutions, with diverse views and strategies). you can do this at all stages of the way\\n\\nwithout inerrupting your main flow, make sure that you as the supervising agent and the sub agents (specially the adversarial ones) make sure whatever theorem is invoke from external papers, you have to check the full text and source of that theorem, read the full context, and then check if the invocation is fully justified, which includes if the obligations of that theorem are truly justified in the way you are invoking it, and if the assertions of the theorems are the only things you use for that invocation and nothing more (you are not assuming anythign the theorem does not assert)\\n\\nYour goal is set to resolving this completely, not stopping until it is resolved one way or the other. \\n\\n use [$solve-math-rigorously](/etc/codex/skills/solve-math-rigorously/SKILL.md)  to solve this rigorously and audit your proof in the end to make sure they are fully valid and correct\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1208\",\"text\":\"I’m continuing under that exact all-datasets quantifier and have recorded it as the active research objective. I’ll keep the affirmative and negative routes separate so that a failure of one proof method is never mistaken for a nonexistence theorem.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1209\",\"summary\":[\"**Planning agent assignments for literature search**\",\"**Planning agent assignments for literature search**\",\"**Deriving scaling constraints for convergence**\",\"**Planning agent assignments for literature search**\",\"**Deriving scaling constraints for convergence**\",\"**Analyzing finite-n gradient flow uniqueness and convergence**\",\"**Planning agent assignments for literature search**\",\"**Deriving scaling constraints for convergence**\",\"**Analyzing finite-n gradient flow uniqueness and convergence**\",\"**Exploring analytic vector field construction via Malliavin calculus**\",\"**Planning agent assignments for literature search**\",\"**Deriving scaling constraints for convergence**\",\"**Analyzing finite-n gradient flow uniqueness and convergence**\",\"**Exploring analytic vector field construction via Malliavin calculus**\",\"**Analyzing kernel block scaling with output weight parameter γ**\",\"**Planning agent assignments for literature search**\",\"**Deriving scaling constraints for convergence**\",\"**Analyzing finite