ueness/restartability.  Until then no \\\\(h_n\\\\) is a proved\\n solution for actual hard ReLU.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1077\",\"text\":\"A new rigorous point emerged for ReLU: although the classical ODE is ill posed, the actual Euler predictor and loss are uniformly equicontinuous for a fixed short time, for every \\\\(\\\\eta(n)\\\\to0\\\\). So ReLU does not share the quadratic “instantaneous jump” obstruction. What remains open is identification and uniqueness of the subsequential generalized limit—not continuity/tightness of its scalar readout.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1078\",\"summary\":[\"**Auditing mean field update bounds**\",\"**Auditing mean field update bounds**\",\"**Verifying update bounds and interpolation properties**\",\"**Auditing mean field update bounds**\",\"**Verifying update bounds and interpolation properties**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-544c954a-34f6-41a4-bf35-c9de86085ee3\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_time_doubling/RELU_NO_CONTINUOUS_LIMIT_TEST.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -194,4 +194,4 @@\\n Let \\\\(h_n\\\\downarrow0\\\\) be arbitrary.  For the width-\\\\(n\\\\) Euler path,\\n-linearly interpolate the grid predictors and losses, calling them\\n-\\\\(F_n\\\\) and \\\\(L_n=(1-F_n)^2/2\\\\).  Then the laws of \\\\(F_n\\\\) and \\\\(L_n\\\\)\\n+linearly interpolate the grid predictors, calling the result \\\\(F_n\\\\), and\\n+put \\\\(L_n=(1-F_n)^2/2\\\\).  Then the laws of \\\\(F_n\\\\) and \\\\(L_n\\\\)\\n are tight in \\\\(C([0,T_0])\\\\).  Every subsequential limit is supported on\\n@@ -230,3 +230,11 @@\\n starts at zero.  The loss assertion follows by the continuous mapping\\n-theorem. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n+theorem.  If instead one linearly interpolates the loss values themselves,\\n+then on a grid cell with endpoint predictors \\\\(x,y\\\\), its difference from\\n+\\\\((1-F_n)^2/2\\\\) is\\n+\\\\[\\n+ {1\\\\over2}\\\\lambda(1-\\\\lambda)(x-y)^2\\n+ \\\\le {1\\\\over8}(x-y)^2.\\n+\\\\]\\n+It is uniformly \\\\(O(h_n^2)\\\\) by (3.4), so the same conclusion holds for\\n+that interpolation. \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-c86dfeb4-d32b-4398-9ab8-ead59bcd240a\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_mesh_resolution/FULL_RESOLUTION_AND_JOINT_SCALING.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -82,4 +82,13 @@\\n \\n-Thus the classical ReLU answer is negative; the generalized hard-Euler\\n-answer is unresolved, not negative.\\n+There is, however, a positive compactness result: for every deterministic\\n+\\\\(h_n\\\\downarrow0\\\\), the scalar Euler predictor and loss are tight in\\n+\\\\(C([0,T_0])\\\\), where\\n+\\\\[\\n+ T_0={1\\\\over384\\\\cdot6^4}.\\n+\\\\]\\n+Every subsequential limit is continuous and starts at output zero and loss\\n+one half.  Thus ReLU has no quadratic-type scalar initial jump on this\\n+interval.  The classical ReLU answer is negative; existence of continuous\\n+subsequences is positive; uniqueness and identification of the generalized\\n+hard-Euler limit remain unresolved.\\n \\n@@ -272,2 +281,29 @@\\n \\n+The scalar local compactness assertion above follows without differentiating\\n+through a gate.  With normalized vector norm \\\\(\\\\|\\\\cdot\\\\|_n\\\\), put\\n+\\\\[\\n+ A=\\\\|a\\\\|_n,\\\\qquad U=\\\\|u\\\\|_n,\\\\qquad\\n+ G=\\\\|W/\\\\sqrt n\\\\|_{\\\\rm op},\\\\qquad R=\\\\max\\\\{1,A,U,G\\\\}.\\n+\\\\]\\n+ReLU Lipschitzness and \\\\(|\\\\phi'|\\\\le\\\\sqrt2\\\\) give for one exact Euler step\\n+\\\\[\\n+ R^+\\\\le R+6hR^5.                                      \\\\tag{5.4}\\n+\\\\]\\n+On the event \\\\(A_0,U_0\\\\le2\\\\), \\\\(G_0\\\\le6\\\\), whose probability tends to one,\\n+(5.4) keeps \\\\(R\\\\le12\\\\) through time\\n+\\\\(T_*=1/(192\\\\cdot6^4)\\\\).  On consecutive states in that ball, a direct\\n+forward calculation gives\\n+\\\\[\\n+ |f^+-f|\\\\le60\\\\cdot12^7h.                              \\\\tag{5.5}\\n+\\\\]\\n+Hence every predictor interpolation is uniformly bounded and\\n+equi-Lipschitz on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\).  Arzelà--Ascoli gives tightness.  Finally,\\n+independence of \\\\(a_0\\\\) from the initialized upper features and RMS\\n+normalization give exactly\\n+\\\\[\\n+ \\\\mathbb E f_n(0)^2={1\\\\over n},\\n+\\\\]\\n+so all subsequential limits have the initialized trace.  The loss follows\\n+by continuous mapping.\\n+\\n At initialization the number of either lower or upper gates in\\n@@ -277,3 +313,3 @@\\n  {N_n(b)\\\\over nh_n}\\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathbb P}b\\\\sqrt{2/\\\\pi}\\n- \\\\quad(nh_n\\\\to\\\\infty),                                 \\\\tag{5.4}\\n+ \\\\quad(nh_n\\\\to\\\\infty),                                 \\\\tag{5.6}\\n \\\\]\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-8a10cdf9-cdc5-4ca5-86d6-3d711c630c4e\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_mesh_resolution/FULL_RESOLUTION_AND_JOINT_SCALING.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -383,4 +383,5 @@\\n joint no-go (2.1); the finite-width classical ReLU attracting-gate\\n-obstruction; the initialization gate-slab law; and the frozen hard-Euler\\n-occupation law.\\n+obstruction; width- and mesh-uniform local tightness of the ReLU scalar\\n+predictor and loss; the initialization gate-slab law; and the frozen\\n+hard-Euler occupation law.\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-ee22c7bd-42b1-44da-8284-6a0a0e27ae97\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_mesh_resolution/EVIDENCE_LEDGER.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -76 +76,16 @@\\n - Claims unchanged: arbitrary under-resolved quadratic diagonals and every canonical hard-ReLU diagonal remain open.\\n+\\n+### C-8: local scalar ReLU joint compactness\\n+\\n+- Statement: For every deterministic `h_n -> 0`, the actual hard-ReLU Euler predictor and loss have continuous initialized subsequential limits on a width-independent positive interval.\\n+- Claim-ladder rung: scalar path tightness and correct initial trace, not identification or uniqueness.\\n+- Status: Proved.\\n+- Explicit interval: `[0,T_0]`, with `T_0=1/(384*6^4)`.\\n+- Supporting evidence: `temporary_loss_time_doubling/RELU_NO_CONTINUOUS_LIMIT_TEST.md`.\\n+- Mechanism: the pathwise recursion `R^+ <= R+6 h R^5` gives a uniform state bound, followed by a width-independent output Lipschitz bound and Arzela--Ascoli; `E f_n(0)^2=1/n`.\\n+- Scope boundary: the theorem does not give a deterministic limit, a unique generalized state, or arbitrary-horizon continuation.\\n+\\n+### Update U-3\\n+\\n+- New evidence: ReLU cannot be assigned the quadratic instantaneous-jump verdict at the scalar level; every mesh sequence is locally precompact with the correct trace.\\n+- Claim refined: C-7 remains open at the uniqueness/identification rung, while C-8 settles local scalar compactness positively.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_7H7lNF3Kqzo0N7qXPessAAg0\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-52d8-7d80-b96b-1da3807d0ee7\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_adversarial_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1079\",\"summary\":[\"**Assessing ReLU theorem extension**\",\"**Evaluating global loss descent for Euler**\",\"**Deriving global output derivative bounds**\",\"**Deriving global output derivative bounds**\",\"**Establishing global parameter norm bounds**\",\"**Deriving global output derivative bounds**\",\"**Establishing global parameter norm bounds**\",\"**Analyzing discrete residual growth and stability**\",\"**Deriving global output derivative bounds**\",\"**Establishing global parameter norm bounds**\",\"**Analyzing discrete residual growth and stability**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ABpVDJGNc11P5NySz70kwVyr\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-52d8-7d80-b96b-1da3807d0ee7\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_adversarial_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1080\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing finite ODE increment behavior**\",\"**Evaluating full mesh uniform convergence failure**\",\"**Analyzing finite ODE increment behavior**\",\"**Evaluating full mesh uniform convergence failure**\",\"**Analyzing finite ODE increment behavior**\",\"**Evaluating full mesh uniform convergence failure**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-d9f777e1-845f-4978-9c9a-573e0fb1ef27\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_mesh_resolution/FULL_RESOLUTION_AND_JOINT_SCALING.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -368,6 +368,20 @@\\n    separate multiplier \\\\(\\\\alpha_n\\\\) to the vector field changes the physical\\n-   clock.  Making it vanish sufficiently fast can produce a trivial frozen\\n-   limit; the critical scale \\\\(\\\\alpha_n\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}\\\\asymp1\\\\) instead zooms\\n-   into the quadratic extreme-coordinate layer and has not been identified\\n-   as an ordinary mean-field flow.\\n+   clock.  For the quadratic model, the deterministic envelopes (4.3) show\\n+   explicitly that\\n+   \\\\[\\n+   \\\\alpha_n n^{14}\\\\longrightarrow0\\n+   \\\\]\\n+   gives the trivial frozen predictor/loss limit on every fixed interval:\\n+   on the high-probability initialization event, the scaled flow stays in\\n+   the radius-\\\\(4n\\\\) ball and\\n+   \\\\[\\n+   \\\\sup_{t\\\\le T}|f_n(t)-f_n(0)|\\n+   \\\\le (20480n^4)(4\\\\cdot10^8n^{10})\\\\,T\\\\alpha_n\\n+   \\\\longrightarrow0.\\n+   \\\\]\\n+   This is a changed, vanishing-speed model, not the original gradient-flow\\n+   clock.  The diagnostic critical scale\\n+   \\\\(\\\\alpha_n\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}\\\\asymp1\\\\) instead zooms into the quadratic\\n+   extreme-coordinate layer and has not been identified as an ordinary\\n+   mean-field flow.\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1081\",\"summary\":[\"**Verifying kernel normalization and residual evolution**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-f935010e-e6eb-41c5-a951-c0895d57970b\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_mesh_resolution/FULL_RESOLUTION_AND_JOINT_SCALING.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -336,3 +336,5 @@\\n \\n-2. **Normalize the optimizer by the tangent kernel.**  Put\\n+2. **Normalize the optimizer by the tangent kernel.**  For the smooth\\n+   quadratic model (and, more generally, along any differentiable trajectory),\\n+   put\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1082\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing activation gradients for mesh guarantee**\",\"**Assessing continuous-time limits for x² and ReLU**\",\"**Analyzing activation gradients for mesh guarantee**\",\"**Assessing continuous-time limits for x² and ReLU**\",\"**Clarifying continuous width limits**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1083\",\"text\":\"The all-\\\\(\\\\eta(n)\\\\) search is now also settled at the correct claim boundary. For \\\\(x^2\\\\), both the true continuous finite-width regime and specially slow width-first diagonals fail, and even a frozen-bottom surrogate fails for every \\\\(\\\\eta(n)\\\\); however, the actual two-layer network still has a mathematically uncontrolled middle scaling because reused-adjoint terms become sign-indefinite. I will not turn that evidence into a universal theorem.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1084\",\"summary\":[\"**Considering adding frozen theorem reference**\",\"**Verifying ReLU gate computations**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_dUvhhLAu9k0wq5GHEfDSLNIy\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-2b64-7d31-9b50-b547408e2b10\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_expansion_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_6btsTEfOEFeMn2YSYOoGa6PO\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-52d8-7d80-b96b-1da3807d0ee7\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_adversarial_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-1085\",\"summary\":[\"**Analyzing interpolation effects on loss crossing**\",\"**Evaluating loss behavior near threshold crossings**\",\"**Planning final report structure**\",\"**Formulating network equations and verdicts**\",\"**Planning final report structure**\",\"**Formulating network equations and verdicts**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-1086\",\"text\":\"## Verdict\\n\\nFor the network\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_n(\\\\theta)\\n=\\n\\\\frac1n\\\\sum_{i=1}^n\\na_i\\\\,\\n\\\\phi\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac1{\\\\sqrt n}\\\\sum_{j=1}^nW_{ij}\\\\phi(u_j)\\n\\\\right),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\ell_n=\\\\frac12(1-f_n)^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith loss-gradient Euler updates\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\theta_{k+1}\\n=\\n\\\\theta_k+\\\\eta(1-f_n(\\\\theta_k))\\\\,n\\\\nabla f_n(\\\\theta_k),\\n\\\\qquad t=k\\\\eta,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe conclusions are:\\n\\n| Activation | Width-first continuous-time limit | Joint \\\\((n,\\\\eta)\\\\) limit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| \\\\(\\\\phi(x)=x^2/\\\\sqrt3\\\\) | **No.** A fixed output/loss change occurs at times tending to zero. | **No for every ODE-resolving scaling.** Arbitrarily under-resolved numerical diagonals remain unclassified. |\\n| \\\\(\\\\phi(x)=\\\\sqrt2\\\\,x_+\\\\) | Classical ODE: **no**, because it is not globally well posed. Euler-selected generalized flow: **open**. | Scalar predictor/loss are locally precompact for every \\\\(\\\\eta(n)\\\\to0\\\\), but uniqueness, identification, and global continuation are open. |\\n\\n### 1. Quadratic: the full mesh guarantee fails\\n\\nLet \\\\(F_k^h\\\\) denote the width-first output. Loss descent is feature ascent with adaptive step\\n\\n\\\\[\\ns_k=h(1-F_k^h).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBefore \\\\(F_k^h\\\\) reaches any fixed \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\in(0,1)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\ns_k\\\\ge (1-\\\\delta)h.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor deterministic nonnegative schedules, the quadratic expected output is coordinatewise monotone in every step size. The audited all-order result gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal F_{2t}\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\rho}{t},\\\\ldots,\\\\frac{\\\\rho}{t}\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow+\\\\infty\\n\\\\qquad(\\\\rho>0).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTaking \\\\(h_N=T/N\\\\) and \\\\(t_N=\\\\lfloor\\\\varepsilon/(2h_N)\\\\rfloor\\\\), monotonicity implies that the loss trajectory must cross \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\) before time \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\), for all sufficiently large \\\\(N\\\\). Hence its first crossing time satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\tau_N(\\\\delta)\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt that time,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF_N(\\\\tau_N)=\\\\delta,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\ell_N(\\\\tau_N)=\\\\frac12(1-\\\\delta)^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhereas initialization gives \\\\(F_N(0)=0\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\ell_N(0)=1/2\\\\). Therefore neither predictor nor loss can converge in \\\\(C([0,T])\\\\) with the initialized trace.\\n\\nThis also shows why the terminal scalar comparison\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\ell_{2t}(\\\\eta)-\\\\ell_t(2\\\\eta)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis insufficient: both meshes could traverse the same initial layer and later agree. The terminal comparison remains unresolved, but the full path guarantee is definitively false.\\n\\n### 2. Quadratic: shrinking \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\) jointly with width does not cure it\\n\\nLet \\\\(f_n^{\\\\mathrm{ode}}\\\\) be the actual finite-width loss flow. There are \\\\(\\\\delta_0>0\\\\) and random times\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\tau_n\\\\le \\\\frac{2}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nsuch that, with probability tending to one,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_n^{\\\\mathrm{ode}}(\\\\tau_n)-f_n^{\\\\mathrm{ode}}(0)\\\\ge\\\\delta_0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nConsequently, every joint scaling satisfying the genuine Euler-consistency condition\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sup_{0\\\\le t\\\\le2/\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}}\\n\\\\left|\\n\\\\bar f_n^{\\\\eta_n}(t)-f_n^{\\\\mathrm{ode}}(t)\\n\\\\right|\\n\\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathbb P}0\\n\\\\]\\n\\ninherits the same vanishing-time jump and cannot converge to a continuous initialized limit.\\n\\nAn explicit sufficient condition for this Euler consistency is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\eta_n n^{14}\\n\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(\\n\\\\frac{6\\\\cdot10^{11}n^9}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\log n}}\\n\\\\right)\\n\\\\longrightarrow0.\\n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor example, \\\\(\\\\eta_n=e^{-10^{12}n^9}\\\\) qualifies—and still fails to produce a continuous initialized limit.\\n\\nThus, under the standard clock \\\\(t=k\\\\eta_n\\\\), making \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\) smaller only resolves the finite-width flow more accurately; it cannot remove the underlying width-dependent initial layer.\\n\\nA deliberately under-resolved intermediate diagonal is not completely ruled out. But any limit obtained there would require an independent consistency theorem and would be a discretization-selected regularization, not presently a joint limit of the canonical finite-width gradient flow.\\n\\n### 3. ReLU: classical flow fails, but continuous Euler subsequences exist\\n\\nAt width two there is an explicit attracting gate with one-sided normal velocities\\n\\n\\\\[\\np=\\\\frac38>0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nq=-\\\\frac{21}{8}<0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth sides point toward the gate. Any fixed choice of \\\\(\\\\phi'(0)\\\\) assigns, generically, a nonzero velocity on the gate, producing no absolutely continuous classical continuation. An open set of Gaussian initializations reaches this obstruction. Hence the fixed-convention classical ReLU gradient ODE is not globally well posed.\\n\\nHowever, this does not imply a discontinuous Euler limit. In fact, for every deterministic \\\\(\\\\eta_n\\\\to0\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR=\\\\max\\\\left\\\\{\\n1,\\\\|a\\\\|_n,\\\\|u\\\\|_n,\\n\\\\left\\\\|\\\\frac W{\\\\sqrt n}\\\\right\\\\|_{\\\\mathrm{op}}\\n\\\\right\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe exact ReLU Euler update obeys\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR^+\\\\le R+6\\\\eta R^5.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nOn a high-probability initialization event, this gives \\\\(R\\\\le12\\\\) on the explicit interval\\n\\n\\\\[\\n0\\\\le t\\\\le T_0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nT_0=\\\\frac1{384\\\\cdot6^4}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nMoreover,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|f^+-f|\\\\le 60\\\\cdot12^7\\\\eta.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore the predictor and loss interpolants are uniformly bounded and equi-Lipschitz on \\\\([0,T_0]\\\\). Their laws are tight in \\\\(C([0,T_0])\\\\), and since\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E f_n(0)^2=\\\\frac1n,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nevery subsequential limit is continuous and begins at\\n\\n\\\\[\\nF(0)=0,\\n\\\\qquad\\nL(0)=\\\\frac12.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo ReLU does not have the quadratic scalar initial-jump obstruction. What remains unproved is whether all subsequences give the same deterministic path and whether the required occupation-augmented OMFP state is unique and restartable.\\n\\nThere is currently no proved \\\\(\\\\eta(n)\\\\) guaranteeing that canonical generalized limit. The initialization slab scale \\\\(n\\\\eta_n\\\\) can be calculated exactly, but it is not sufficient: attracting gates create temporal Young measures whose joint correlations are visible to reused adjoints and squared tangent-kernel terms.\\n\\n### 4. Ways to obtain an actual continuous limit\\n\\nThree mathematically valid modifications are available:\\n\\n1. **Use normalized arctangent.**  \\n   For the same depth-two feature-learning scaling, normalized \\\\(\\\\arctan\\\\) has a proved unique global restartable pointed-action IDE, with compact-time convergence of predictors, tangent kernels, and losses.\\n\\n2. **Kernel-normalize the quadratic optimizer.**  \\n   With\\n   \\\\[\\n   K_n=Df_n[g_n]=\\\\frac1n\\\\|g_n\\\\|_2^2,\\n   \\\\qquad\\n   \\\\dot\\\\theta=\\\\frac{(1-f_n)g_n}{K_n},\\n   \\\\]\\n   one obtains exactly\\n   \\\\[\\n   \\\\dot f_n=1-f_n,\\\\qquad\\n   f_n(t)=1-(1-f_n(0))e^{-t},\\\\qquad\\n   \\\\ell_n(t)=\\\\ell_n(0)e^{-2t}.\\n   \\\\]\\n   This fixes the predictor/loss clock, although it is a different optimizer.\\n\\n3. **Slow the physical vector field separately.**  \\n   Multiplying the quadratic vector field by \\\\(\\\\alpha_n\\\\) with\\n   \\\\[\\n   \\\\alpha_n n^{14}\\\\to0\\n   \\\\]\\n   produces a rigorously frozen predictor/loss limit. This is trivial and changes physical time; it is not achieved merely by shrinking the Euler mesh.\\n\\nThe complete audited synthesis is in [FULL_RESOLUTION_AND_JOINT_SCALING.md](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_mesh_resolution/FULL_RESOLUTION_AND_JOINT_SCALING.md). Detailed proofs are in [JOINT_SCALING_VERDICT.md](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_joint_width_mesh_quadratic/JOINT_SCALING_VERDICT.md) and [RELU_NO_CONTINUOUS_LIMIT_TEST.md](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_time_doubling/RELU_NO_CONTINUOUS_LIMIT_TEST.md).\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a03a63-b192-7db2-baf3-426da385d235\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1787686007,\"completedAt\":1787686579,\"durationMs\":571796,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"item-908\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"what happens if we switch to gradient of loss instead of feature ascent time, meaning, we have a single sample with y=1, what happens to the discrepancy of loss l_2t(\\\\eta)-l_t(2\\\\eta)? can you calculate the expansion up to sufficient terms to argue definately if it satisfies the continuous time limit or not? \\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-909\",\"summary\":[\"**Interpreting gradient dynamics for square loss**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-910\",\"text\":\"I’ll analyze the square-loss dynamics as a separate Euler scheme, not by simply replacing \\\\(h\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\eta(1-F)\\\\): the residual is recomputed after every step and is correlated with the network state. I’m using the conjecture-investigation workflow to identify the decisive expansion, then the rigorous-math workflow to audit the limit order and the continuous-time conclusion. I’ll remain inside the MFP study folder.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_Bzk0yFwPqjM3SkauhLP5y0YZ\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-2b64-7d31-9b50-b547408e2b10\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_expansion_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_D7g9cRUruo3wlwu3jL7UomH9\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-3cd0-7612-91ca-3c0dab5ae6ab\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_timechange_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_5cXpQ61FnHMLwHnlsMRIsxKU\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-52d8-7d80-b96b-1da3807d0ee7\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_adversarial_audit\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-911\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying loss assumption and update formula**\",\"**Clarifying loss assumption and update formula**\",\"**Analyzing Euler step discrepancy in loss flow**\",\"**Clarifying loss assumption and update formula**\",\"**Analyzing Euler step discrepancy in loss flow**\",\"**Evaluating continuous-time limit conditions with pathological activations**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving loss expansion through third order terms**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving loss expansion through third order terms**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving loss expansion through third order terms**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving loss expansion through third order terms**\",\"**Computing third-order loss expansion coefficients symbolically**\",\"**Deriving B-series expansion for vector field**\",\"**Expanding Euler step with temporal polynomials**\",\"**Analyzing loss discrepancy with ReLU kinks**\",\"**Calculating loss discrepancy coefficients including kinks**\",\"**Estimating continuous-time limit consistency despite kinks**\",\"**Deriving loss expansion through third order terms**\",\"**Computing third-order loss expansion coefficients symbolically**\",\"**Verifying population derivative assumptions**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-912\",\"text\":\"For the conventional squared loss\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathcal L(f)=\\\\tfrac12(1-f)^2,\\n\\\\]\\nthe first structural change is already clear: loss descent is the Euler map for\\n\\\\[\\nV(\\\\theta)=(1-f(\\\\theta))\\\\,\\\\nabla f(\\\\theta).\\n\\\\]\\nThe target \\\\(y=1\\\\) breaks the readout parity that removed the quadratic defect in feature ascent. Consequently the loss refinement discrepancy generically starts at order \\\\(\\\\eta^2\\\\), not \\\\(\\\\eta^3\\\\) or \\\\(\\\\eta^5\\\\). I’m deriving its explicit activation constants and checking what that order does—and does not—imply when \\\\(t\\\\eta\\\\) is fixed.\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-913\",\"summary\":[\"**Deriving universal expansion coefficients**\",\"**Deriving universal expansion coefficients**\",\"**Proving finite expansion insufficiency**\",\"**Deriving universal expansion coefficients**\",\"**Proving finite expansion insufficiency**\",\"**Deriving activation integral invariants**\",\"**Deriving universal expansion coefficients**\",\"**Proving finite expansion insufficiency**\",\"**Deriving activation integral invariants**\",\"**Deriving universal expansion coefficients**\",\"**Proving finite expansion insufficiency**\",\"**Deriving activation integral invariants**\",\"**Deriving loss-feature ascent relation**\",\"**Deriving loss-feature ascent relation**\",\"**Validating cubic loss expansion**\",\"**Deriving loss-feature ascent relation**\",\"**Validating cubic loss expansion**\",\"**Deriving loss-feature ascent relation**\",\"**Validating cubic loss expansion**\",\"**Establishing finite expansion limitation rigorously**\",\"**Deriving loss-feature ascent relation**\",\"**Validating cubic loss expansion**\",\"**Establishing finite expansion limitation rigorously**\",\"**Proving uniform loss bound under smoothness assumptions**\",\"**Deriving full loss expansion from potential**\",\"**Deriving full loss expansion from potential**\",\"**Expanding loss terms via potential derivatives**\",\"**Verifying order5 recurrence notation**\",\"**Verifying order5 recurrence notation**\",\"**Confirming operator commutation and coefficient formulas**\",\"**Verifying order5 recurrence notation**\",\"**Confirming operator commutation and coefficient formulas**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_aPR5UiARehYHZ75CxyuPPFIS\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-52d8-7d80-b96b-1da3807d0ee7\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_adversarial_audit\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-54461577-2f12-4473-a26a-e02e354ad9c2\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_ORDER5_AND_MESH_STATUS.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# One-sample square loss: exact order-five paired expansion and mesh status\\n\\n## 1. Convention and exact reduction\\n\\nUse the half-square loss\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\ell(\\\\theta)=\\\\frac12(1-f(\\\\theta))^2\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the same mean-field-scaled parameter metric as in the feature-ascent\\nstudy.  One gradient-descent step is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\theta^+=\\\\theta-\\\\eta\\\\nabla\\\\ell(\\\\theta)\\n =\\\\theta+\\\\eta(1-f(\\\\theta))\\\\nabla f(\\\\theta).\\n \\\\tag{1.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nPut\\n\\n\\\\[\\n P(\\\\theta)=f(\\\\theta)-\\\\frac12f(\\\\theta)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen `ell=1/2-P`, and (1.1) is exactly one gradient-ascent Euler step for\\n`P`.  This is a state-dependent time rescaling of feature ascent, not a\\nconstant replacement of the learning rate.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n v=\\\\nabla P=(1-f)\\\\nabla f,\\n \\\\qquad E_hx=x+hv(x),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand define\\n\\n\\\\[\\n D_t^\\\\ell(h)=\\\\ell(E_h^{2t}x_0)-\\\\ell(E_{2h}^{t}x_0).\\n \\\\tag{1.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor the width-first network, every statement below is applied only after\\nthe fixed-step population DAG and the required local regularity have been\\nidentified.  No finite-width Taylor expansion is interchanged with width.\\n\\n## 2. Exact order-five pullback formula\\n\\nFor a scalar test function `u`, define the differential operators\\n\\n\\\\[\\n T_ku={1\\\\over k!}D^ku[v,\\\\ldots,v],\\\\qquad k\\\\ge1.\\n \\\\tag{2.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor `1<=r<=m`, put\\n\\n\\\\[\\n W_{m,r}\\n =\\\\sum_{\\\\substack{k_1+\\\\cdots+k_r=m\\\\\\\\k_i\\\\ge1}}\\n T_{k_1}\\\\cdots T_{k_r},\\n \\\\qquad\\n \\\\Lambda_{m,r}=(W_{m,r}\\\\ell)(x_0),\\n \\\\tag{2.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n q_{m,r}(t)={2t\\\\choose r}-2^m{t\\\\choose r}.\\n \\\\tag{2.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf the population DAG is `C^6` in `h` near zero, then\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\boxed{\\n D_t^\\\\ell(h)\\n =\\\\sum_{m=2}^{5}h^m\\\\sum_{r=1}^m\\n q_{m,r}(t)\\\\Lambda_{m,r}+R_{6,t}(h).}\\n \\\\tag{2.4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nHere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n R_{6,t}(h)\\n ={h^6\\\\over5!}\\\\int_0^1(1-s)^5\\n {d^6\\\\over dh^6}D_t^\\\\ell(sh)\\\\,ds.\\n \\\\tag{2.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe nonzero rows of (2.3) are\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\\nm\\\\backslash r&1&2&3&4&5\\\\\\\\ \\\\hline\\n2&-2t&t\\\\\\\\[2pt]\\n3&-6t&-2t^2+3t&2t(t-1)\\\\\\\\[2pt]\\n4&-14t&-6t^2+7t&-\\\\frac43t^3+6t^2-\\\\frac{14}3t\\n &2t^3-\\\\frac{11}2t^2+\\\\frac72t\\\\\\\\[2pt]\\n5&-30t&-14t^2+15t&-4t^3+14t^2-10t\\n &-\\\\frac23t^4+6t^3-\\\\frac{77}6t^2+\\\\frac{15}2t\\n &\\\\frac43t^4-7t^3+\\\\frac{35}3t^2-6t.\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\tag{2.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n### Proof\\n\\nThe one-step pullback is the exact Taylor operator\\n\\n\\\\[\\n (U_hu)(x)=u(x+hv(x))\\n =\\\\left(I+\\\\sum_{k\\\\ge1}h^kT_k\\\\right)u(x).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWriting `A_h=sum_(k>=1)h^kT_k`, the identical one-step operator occurs at\\nevery time, so\\n\\n\\\\[\\n U_h^N=(I+A_h)^N=\\\\sum_{r=0}^N{N\\\\choose r}A_h^r.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe coefficient of `h^m` in `A_h^r` is exactly `W_(m,r)`.  Subtracting\\n`U_(2h)^t` from `U_h^(2t)` gives (2.3)--(2.4).  Direct substitution into\\n(2.3) gives the finite table (2.6).  Taylor's integral theorem gives\\n(2.5).  This proof terminates after the displayed five rows.\\n\\nThe operators in (2.2) are evaluated at initialization.  In the established\\nsmooth OMFP calculus, source differentiation and Gaussian peeling reduce\\neach `Lambda_(m,r)` to a terminating Gaussian activation integral.  Thus\\nthey are not constants defined from a trained trajectory or output\\nsupremum.\\n\\n## 3. Explicit quadratic and cubic terms at depth two\\n\\nAt initialization let\\n\\n\\\\[\\n g=\\\\nabla f,\\\\qquad\\n \\\\Theta=\\\\|g\\\\|^2,qquad\\n \\\\mathsf H=\\\\|Dg[g]\\\\|^2,qquad\\n \\\\mathsf S=D^3f[g,g,g].\\n \\\\tag{3.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nReadout reflection gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n D^2f[g,g]=0.\\n \\\\tag{3.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe first two rows of (2.4) reduce to\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\boxed{\\n D_t^\\\\ell(h)\\n =t\\\\Theta^2h^2\\n -{t(2t-1)\\\\over2}\\n \\\\{4\\\\Theta^3+\\\\mathsf S+4\\\\mathsf H\\\\}h^3\\n +K_{4,\\\\phi}(t)h^4+K_{5,\\\\phi}(t)h^5+R_{6,t}(h),}\\n \\\\tag{3.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n K_{m,\\\\phi}(t)=\\\\sum_{r=1}^mq_{m,r}(t)\\\\Lambda_{m,r},\\n \\\\qquad m=4,5.\\n \\\\tag{3.4}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIn particular, `deg K_(4,phi)<=3` and `deg K_(5,phi)<=4`.\\n\\nFor the `q=1`, two-hidden-layer network,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\Theta=1+d+d^2,qquad d=\\\\mathbb E\\\\phi'(G)^2.\\n \\\\tag{3.5}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe constants `mathsf S,mathsf H` are the already established two-hidden\\nlayer cubic invariants.  To make their activation provenance explicit, set\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{array}{lll}\\nd=\\\\mathbb E p^2,&u=\\\\mathbb E p^4,&v=\\\\mathbb E[g_0q],\\\\\\\\\\nm=\\\\mathbb E[g_0p^2q],&r=\\\\mathbb E[pr_3],&s=\\\\mathbb E q^2,\\\\\\\\\\nj=\\\\mathbb E[p^3r_3],&e=\\\\mathbb E[p^2q^2],\\n&\\\\ell_0=\\\\mathbb E[g_0^2p^2],\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\tag{3.6}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere `G~N(0,1)`, `g_0=phi(G)`, `p=phi'(G)`, `q=phi''(G)`, and\\n`r_3=phi'''(G)`.  Put\\n\\n\\\\[\\n c=1+d,\\\\qquad \\\\beta=v+cr,\\\\qquad\\n \\\\delta=d+cs,\\\\qquad k=d+\\\\beta+\\\\delta.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\mathsf S\\n =3c^2m+3c^3j+3du\\\\beta+3dkm+3d^2j,\\n \\\\tag{3.7}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\n \\\\mathsf H={}&c^2u+c\\\\ell_0+2c^2m+3c^3e+cuds\\n +2ud^2+3d^2e\\\\\\\\\\n &+k^2\\\\ell_0+2dkm.\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\tag{3.8}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus every displayed coefficient through cubic order is an explicit finite\\nformula in Gaussian activation moments.  Equations (2.2), (2.6), and the\\nfinite source-peeling compiler give the same property at orders four and\\nfive.\\n\\n## 4. Hard ReLU/leaky-ReLU candidate\\n\\nFor normalized ReLU or conventional normalized leaky ReLU, `d=1`, hence\\n`Theta=3`.  The smooth `C^6` formula is not applicable at the kink.  If the\\ntwo still-open indicator-DAG and marked-source intertwining bridges from\\n`RELU_LEAKY_FINAL_STATUS.md` are assumed, the previously computed gate\\ncoefficient changes sign because `ell'(0)=-1`.  Consequently\\n\\n\\\\[\\n D_t^\\\\ell(h)\\n =9t h^2-tD_{a,b}h|h|+o_t(h^2).\\n \\\\tag{4.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor positive `h`, this is `t(9-D_(a,b))h^2+o_t(h^2)`.  Formula (4.1) is\\nconditional; loss training does not close either hard-kink bridge.\\n\\n## 5. Why no finite expansion decides mesh removal\\n\\nAt every fixed order `m`, (2.3) has degree at most `m-1` in `t`: the\\ndegree-`m` terms in `{2t choose r}` and `2^m{t choose r}` cancel after all\\n`r` are assembled, equivalently because equal physical time cancels the\\nexact-flow principal term.  Therefore, at `T=2th`, every fixed-order term\\nin (2.4) is at most\\n\\n\\\\[\\n C_{m,\\\\phi,T}t^{m-1}h^m=O_{m,\\\\phi,T}(t^{-1}).\\n \\\\tag{5.1}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis includes the hard-kink term in (4.1).  The uncontrolled integral\\nremainder (2.5), or an all-source nonanalytic tail, can nevertheless fail\\nto be uniform in `t`.  Hence no finite jet, including the complete order-five\\njet, proves or disproves a continuous-time limit.\\n\\nA sufficient state estimate is the following.  On an activation-envelope\\ndefined reachable set through time `T`, suppose\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\|v\\\\|\\\\le M_T,\\\\qquad\\n \\\\|v(x)-v(y)\\\\|\\\\le L_T\\\\|x-y\\\\|,qquad\\n |\\\\ell(x)-\\\\ell(y)|\\\\le R_T\\\\|x-y\\\\|.\\n \\\\tag{5.2}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen the exact one-pair state defect is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n E_h^2x-E_{2h}x=h\\\\{v(x+hv(x))-v(x)\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand the hybrid telescope gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n \\\\boxed{\\n |D_t^\\\\ell(h)|\\n \\\\le R_TL_TM_T\\\\,t h^2e^{2L_Tt|h|}.}\\n \\\\tag{5.3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor `h=T/(2t)`, the right side is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n {R_TL_TM_TT^2e^{L_TT}\\\\over4t},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhich is summable along dyadic refinements.  This proves scalar dyadic mesh\\nconvergence, and the analogous state bound plus generator consistency proves\\nthe population flow.\\n\\nFor nonlinear reused-matrix OMFP, (5.2) has not been derived from activation\\nenvelopes on a restartable reachable set.  Defining its constants by a\\nsupremum over the unknown trained trajectory would be circular.  Exact\\nReLU/leaky-ReLU also lack a classical Lipschitz vector field at their gate\\nsurfaces.  Therefore the current rigorous verdict is:\\n\\n* the loss discrepancy is compatible with an ordinary first-order\\n  continuous-time limit;\\n* neither the quadratic kink term nor the super-fifth feature-ascent\\n  construction refutes that limit;\\n* the actual nonlinear width-first loss-gradient flow remains open until a\\n  reachable state stability estimate such as (5.2), or a proved Osgood/BV\\n  replacement, is supplied.\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-fa41d7e0-bc44-473e-a3d3-b49e093671e1\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_ORDER5_AND_MESH_STATUS.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -215,5 +215,5 @@\\n \\\\begin{aligned}\\n- \\\\mathsf H={}&c^2u+c\\\\ell_0+2c^2m+3c^3e+cuds\\n+ \\\\mathsf H={}&c^2u+c\\\\ell_0+2c^2m+3c^3e+c\\\\,u\\\\,d\\\\,s\\n  +2ud^2+3d^2e\\\\\\\\\\n- &+k^2\\\\ell_0+2dkm.\\n+ &+k^2\\\\ell_0+2d\\\\,k\\\\,m.\\n \\\\end{aligned}\\n@@ -310,2 +310 @@\\n   replacement, is supplied.\\n-\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-914\",\"summary\":[\"**Clarifying full MSE factor mapping**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-a0f2c9f7-3758-4a6c-86c6-f9495ac89490\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_time_doubling/LOSS_TIMECHANGE_ANALYSIS.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -230,3 +230,4 @@\\n \\\\[\\n- \\\\phi_{a,b}(x)=ax_++bx_-,\\\\qquad {a^2+b^2\\\\over2}=1,\\n+ \\\\phi_{a,b}(x)=ax_++bx_-,\\\\qquad x_-:=\\\\min\\\\{x,0\\\\},\\n+ \\\\qquad {a^2+b^2\\\\over2}=1,\\n  \\\\quad 0\\\\le b<a.                                          \\\\tag{5.1}\\n@@ -315,3 +316,3 @@\\n  \\\\boxed{\\n- |d_t(h)|\\\\le L_TG_T^2,t h^2e^{2L_Tt h},\\n+ |d_t(h)|\\\\le L_TG_T^2\\\\,t h^2e^{2L_Tt h},\\n  \\\\qquad 2th\\\\le T.}                                       \\\\tag{6.3}\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-bdf981bf-631b-46c1-8714-4abc80b367ce\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_ORDER5_AND_MESH_STATUS.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"update\",\"move_path\":null},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"@@ -11,3 +11,5 @@\\n and the same mean-field-scaled parameter metric as in the feature-ascent\\n-study.  One gradient-descent step is\\n+study.  The calculation assumes the established fixed, flat population\\n+metric/adjoint realization; this identification is part of the OMFP bridge,\\n+not a formal change of coordinates.  One gradient-descent step is\\n \\n@@ -74,3 +76,4 @@\\n \\n-If the population DAG is `C^6` in `h` near zero, then\\n+If the population DAG is `C^6` on the complete step segment used below,\\n+then\\n \\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-e8c726eb-b0e6-4e4e-8512-4dcbd30e6a17\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_ORDER5_INDEPENDENT_AUDIT.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Independent audit of the loss order-five formula\\n\\n## Verdict\\n\\nThe pullback algebra, every temporal coefficient through order five, the\\nquadratic/cubic reduction, and the integral remainder in\\n`LOSS_ORDER5_AND_MESH_STATUS.md` pass.\\n\\nThe result is a fixed-step width-first jet theorem only under the stated\\nregularity and OMFP-intertwining hypotheses.  It supplies no uniform\\ncontinuous-time theorem without an activation-envelope-derived bound on the\\nsixth derivative or, preferably, the state-level reachable stability bound.\\n\\n## Algebra check\\n\\nWith\\n\\n\\\\[\\n U_h=I+A_h,\\\\qquad A_h=\\\\sum_{k\\\\ge1}h^kT_k,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe binomial theorem is valid because there is one operator `A_h`:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n [h^m]U_h^N\\n =\\\\sum_{r=1}^m{N\\\\choose r}\\n \\\\sum_{k_1+\\\\cdots+k_r=m}T_{k_1}\\\\cdots T_{k_r}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nTherefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n q_{m,r}(t)={2t\\\\choose r}-2^m{t\\\\choose r}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIndependent rational expansion of this expression reproduces every entry\\nof the rows `m=2,3,4,5` in the main note.\\n\\n## Low-order contraction check\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\n A=\\\\|\\\\nabla f\\\\|^2,qquad\\n C=D^3f[g,g,g],qquad\\n H=\\\\|Dg[g]\\\\|^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand use the initialization parity `D^2f[g,g]=0`.  Direct differentiation of\\n`P=f-f^2/2` and `ell=1/2-P` gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{array}{lll}\\n \\\\Lambda_{2,1}=A^2/2,&\\\\quad&\\\\Lambda_{2,2}=2A^2,\\\\\\\\\\n \\\\Lambda_{3,1}=-C/6,&&\\n \\\\Lambda_{3,2}=-2A^3-3C/2-2H,\\\\\\\\\\n &&\\\\Lambda_{3,3}=-4A^3-2C-4H.\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nContracting these values against the audited `q` rows yields\\n\\n\\\\[\\n [h^2]D_t^\\\\ell=tA^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\n\\\\[\\n [h^3]D_t^\\\\ell\\n =-{t(2t-1)\\\\over2}(4A^3+C+4H),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith the same sign and fine-minus-coarse orientation as the main note.\\n\\n## Remainder and claim boundary\\n\\nTaylor's theorem gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n R_{6,t}(h)\\n ={h^6\\\\over5!}\\\\int_0^1(1-s)^5\\n (D_t^\\\\ell)^{(6)}(sh)\\\\,ds\\n\\\\]\\n\\nprovided the paired scalar map is `C^6` on the entire segment.  Fixed-`t`\\nregularity does not bound this integral uniformly when `t` grows like\\n`1/h`.  Width-first use additionally requires direct population-DAG\\nregularity and uniform integrability; finite-width Taylor expansion cannot\\nbe interchanged with width.  Hard ReLU/leaky-ReLU are outside the `C^6`\\nhypothesis and require the separately open boundary calculus.\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-915\",\"summary\":[\"**Confirming polynomial degree and expansion limits**\",\"**Confirming polynomial degree and expansion limits**\",\"**Defining and simplifying order-5 expansion terms**\",\"**Confirming polynomial degree and expansion limits**\",\"**Defining and simplifying order-5 expansion terms**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_lxdmW4IapzHtN2GBqQhTmHRI\",\"kind\":\"interrupted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-52d8-7d80-b96b-1da3807d0ee7\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_adversarial_audit\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_ns8Rom7HMsWiRMQftMjDBuv8\",\"kind\":\"interrupted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-2b64-7d31-9b50-b547408e2b10\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_expansion_route\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_Q1aztBmo9MMvLVHcYMP8LW9X\",\"kind\":\"interrupted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a03a64-3cd0-7612-91ca-3c0dab5ae6ab\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/loss_timechange_route\"},{\"type\":\"fileChange\",\"id\":\"exec-00b35493-95ff-4fcf-807c-2a66f45f2e63\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/EVIDENCE_LEDGER.md\",\"kind\":{\"type\":\"add\"},\"diff\":{\"text\":\"# Evidence ledger: one-sample loss-gradient time doubling\\n\\n### C-1: finite-width potential reduction\\n\\n- Statement: half-MSE descent is exactly Euler ascent for\\n  `P=f-f^2/2`, and `ell=1/2-P`.\\n- Status: Proved.\\n- Evidence: algebraic identity in `LOSS_TIMECHANGE_ANALYSIS.md` Section 1.\\n- Dependencies: none.\\n\\n### C-2: smooth order-five width-first expansion\\n\\n- Statement: conditional on fixed-step DAG identification, flat population\\n  metric/adjoint intertwining, `C^6` segment regularity, and UI, equation\\n  (2.4) of `LOSS_ORDER5_AND_MESH_STATUS.md` is the exact expansion.\\n- Status: Exact-under-assumptions.\\n- Evidence: pullback binomial proof; independent audit of every rational\\n  coefficient in `LOSS_ORDER5_INDEPENDENT_AUDIT.md`.\\n- Concrete falsifier: failure of the stated DAG regularity or metric\\n  identification.\\n\\n### C-3: hard ReLU/leaky leading coefficient\\n\\n- Statement: if the two open hard-kink OMFP bridges hold, then\\n  `D_t^ell(h)=9t h^2-tD_(a,b)h|h|+o_t(h^2)`.\\n- Status: Exact-under-assumptions; actual-network claim open.\\n- Supporting evidence: exact scalar boundary identity, reused-field candidate,\\n  and observable-sign reduction.\\n- Missing dependencies: indicator-valued fixed-step width identification and\\n  complete marked-source response intertwining.\\n\\n### C-4: finite jets decide continuous time\\n\\n- Statement: some finite Taylor order, including order five, proves or\\n  disproves mesh removal.\\n- Status: Falsified as a logical implication.\\n- Evidence: every order-`m` paired temporal coefficient has degree at most\\n  `m-1` in `t`, so every fixed order is `O(1/t)` at `h=T/(2t)`, while the\\n  all-order remainder remains uncontrolled.\\n\\n### C-5: reachable Lipschitz sewing\\n\\n- Statement: activation-envelope-derived reachable bounds\\n  `||v||<=M_T`, `Lip(v)<=L_T`, and `Lip(ell)<=R_T` imply\\n  `|D_t^ell(h)|<=R_TL_TM_T t h^2 exp(2L_Tt|h|)` and dyadic convergence.\\n- Status: Proved conditional implication.\\n- Remaining obligation: establish the bounds noncircularly for nonlinear\\n  reused-matrix OMFP, or prove an Osgood/BV replacement.\\n\\n## Current bottom line\\n\\nThe loss discrepancy has ordinary first-order Euler scaling and contains no\\nproved continuous-time obstruction.  The actual nonlinear width-first\\nReLU/leaky-ReLU loss-gradient flow remains open, not disproved.\\n\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"item-916\",\"summary\":[\"**Finalizing loss formula explanation**\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"item-917\",\"text\":\"Assuming the conventional half-squared loss\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\ell(f)=\\\\frac12(1-f)^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nloss descent changes the picture substantially: the fine/coarse discrepancy generically begins at order \\\\(\\\\eta^2\\\\). This is ordinary first-order Euler error and is compatible with a continuous-time limit.\\n\\n### Exact reduction\\n\\nDefine\\n\\n\\\\[\\nP(\\\\theta)=f(\\\\theta)-\\\\frac12f(\\\\theta)^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen\\n\\n\\\\[\\n-\\\\nabla\\\\ell=(1-f)\\\\nabla f=\\\\nabla P,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\ell=\\\\frac12-P.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThus one loss-gradient step is exactly Euler ascent for \\\\(P\\\\):\\n\\n\\\\[\\nE_\\\\eta(\\\\theta)=\\\\theta+\\\\eta\\\\nabla P(\\\\theta).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nLet\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD_t^\\\\ell(\\\\eta)\\n=\\n\\\\ell(E_\\\\eta^{2t}\\\\theta_0)\\n-\\n\\\\ell(E_{2\\\\eta}^{t}\\\\theta_0).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is not constant-step feature ascent: the effective feature step \\\\(\\\\eta(1-f)\\\\) is recomputed at every iteration.\\n\\n### Complete expansion through order five\\n\\nFor a scalar function \\\\(u\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT_k u=\\\\frac1{k!}D^ku[v,\\\\ldots,v],\\n\\\\qquad\\nv=\\\\nabla P,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Lambda_{m,r}\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{\\\\substack{k_1+\\\\cdots+k_r=m\\\\\\\\k_i\\\\ge1}}\\n(T_{k_1}\\\\cdots T_{k_r}\\\\ell)(\\\\theta_0).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen, whenever the width-first population DAG is \\\\(C^6\\\\) on the required segment,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\nD_t^\\\\ell(\\\\eta)\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{m=2}^{5}\\\\eta^m\\n\\\\sum_{r=1}^{m}\\n\\\\left[\\n{2t\\\\choose r}\\n-\\n2^m{t\\\\choose r}\\n\\\\right]\\\\Lambda_{m,r}\\n+\\nR_{6,t}(\\\\eta),\\n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwith the exact integral remainder\\n\\n\\\\[\\nR_{6,t}(\\\\eta)\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\eta^6}{5!}\\n\\\\int_0^1(1-s)^5\\n\\\\frac{d^6}{d\\\\eta^6}D_t^\\\\ell(s\\\\eta)\\\\,ds.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEvery \\\\(\\\\Lambda_{m,r}\\\\) is an initialization quantity and can be reduced by Gaussian peeling; it is not defined from the trained trajectory.\\n\\nFor the \\\\(q=1,L=2\\\\) network, let\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\phi'(G)^2],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\Theta=1+d+d^2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nand let the already established cubic invariants be\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathsf S_{\\\\phi,2}\\n=\\nD^3f[g,g,g],\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\mathsf H_{\\\\phi,2}\\n=\\n\\\\|Dg[g]\\\\|^2,\\n\\\\qquad g=\\\\nabla f.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nReadout symmetry gives \\\\(D^2f[g,g]=0\\\\) at initialization. Hence\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n\\\\begin{aligned}\\nD_t^\\\\ell(\\\\eta)\\n={}&t\\\\Theta^2\\\\eta^2\\\\\\\\\\n&-\\\\frac{t(2t-1)}2\\n\\\\left(\\n4\\\\Theta^3+\\\\mathsf S_{\\\\phi,2}+4\\\\mathsf H_{\\\\phi,2}\\n\\\\right)\\\\eta^3\\\\\\\\\\n&+K_{4,\\\\phi}(t)\\\\eta^4\\n+K_{5,\\\\phi}(t)\\\\eta^5\\n+R_{6,t}(\\\\eta),\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\deg K_{4,\\\\phi}\\\\le3,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\deg K_{5,\\\\phi}\\\\le4.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe explicit Gaussian-integral formulas for \\\\(\\\\mathsf S_{\\\\phi,2}\\\\), \\\\(\\\\mathsf H_{\\\\phi,2}\\\\), \\\\(K_{4,\\\\phi}\\\\), and \\\\(K_{5,\\\\phi}\\\\) are recorded in the study note linked below.\\n\\n### ReLU and leaky-ReLU\\n\\nFor normalized ReLU/leaky-ReLU,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nd=1,\\\\qquad \\\\Theta=3.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe smooth formula is not directly valid at the kink. Conditional on the same two open hard-kink OMFP bridges identified previously, the Gaussian boundary term changes sign because \\\\(\\\\ell'(0)=-1\\\\). This gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\nD_t^\\\\ell(\\\\eta)\\n=\\n9t\\\\eta^2\\n-\\ntD_{a,b}\\\\eta|\\\\eta|\\n+\\no_t(\\\\eta^2),\\n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD_{a,b}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{3(a-b)}{4\\\\pi\\\\sqrt e}\\n\\\\int_{2b}^{2a}\\n\\\\frac{(y-2b)(2a-y)}\\n     {(1+y^2/e)^{5/2}}\\\\,dy,\\n\\\\qquad\\ne=\\\\frac{a^4+b^4}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor positive \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD_t^\\\\ell(\\\\eta)\\n=\\nt(9-D_{a,b})\\\\eta^2+o_t(\\\\eta^2).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor ReLU,\\n\\n\\\\[\\nD_{\\\\mathrm{ReLU}}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt2}{\\\\pi}\\n\\\\left(1-\\\\frac1{\\\\sqrt5}\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\n\\nso the candidate coefficient is\\n\\n\\\\[\\n9-D_{\\\\mathrm{ReLU}}\\n\\\\approx 8.75115869.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis hard-ReLU formula remains conditional for the actual width-first network.\\n\\n### Does this violate the continuous-time limit?\\n\\nNo. At fixed physical time\\n\\n\\\\[\\nT=2t\\\\eta,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nthe leading loss discrepancy is\\n\\n\\\\[\\nt\\\\eta^2=\\\\frac{T^2}{4t}=O(t^{-1}).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nMore generally, Euler chronology implies that the order-\\\\(\\\\eta^m\\\\) paired coefficient has degree at most \\\\(m-1\\\\) in \\\\(t\\\\). Therefore every fixed Taylor order satisfies\\n\\n\\\\[\\nt^{m-1}\\\\eta^m=O(t^{-1})\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\left(\\\\eta=\\\\frac{T}{2t}\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nSo neither the ReLU kink nor the previously constructed super-large fifth coefficient is a continuous-time obstruction.\\n\\nBut no finite expansion—even the complete fifth-order expansion—proves convergence, because \\\\(R_{6,t}\\\\) may be nonuniform in \\\\(t\\\\).\\n\\nA sufficient noncircular state estimate would be: on an activation-envelope-certified reachable set,\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\|v\\\\|\\\\le M_T,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Lip}(v)\\\\le L_T,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{Lip}(\\\\ell)\\\\le R_T.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen an exact hybrid telescoping argument gives\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{\\n|D_t^\\\\ell(\\\\eta)|\\n\\\\le\\nR_TL_TM_T\\\\,\\nt\\\\eta^2e^{2L_Tt|\\\\eta|}.\\n}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAt \\\\(\\\\eta=T/(2t)\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\n|D_t^\\\\ell(T/(2t))|\\n\\\\le\\n\\\\frac{R_TL_TM_TT^2e^{L_TT}}{4t},\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhich is summable along dyadic refinements and proves scalar mesh convergence.\\n\\nThose reachable-state bounds have not yet been proved for nonlinear reused-matrix OMFP, and classical Lipschitz control does not apply directly to hard ReLU gates. Therefore the definitive conclusion is:\\n\\n- the loss discrepancy is consistent with a normal first-order continuous-time limit;\\n- it contains no proved obstruction;\\n- the actual nonlinear width-first ReLU/leaky-ReLU loss-gradient limit remains open, because an all-source reachable stability theorem is still missing.\\n\\nIf you use the unhalved loss \\\\((1-f)^2\\\\), replace \\\\(\\\\eta\\\\) by \\\\(2\\\\eta\\\\) and multiply the displayed loss discrepancy by \\\\(2\\\\).\\n\\nFiles:\\n\\n- [Order-five expansion and mesh status](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_ORDER5_AND_MESH_STATUS.md)\\n- [Independent order-five audit](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_ORDER5_INDEPENDENT_AUDIT.md)\\n- [Time-change analysis](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_time_doubling/LOSS_TIMECHANGE_ANALYSIS.md)\\n- [Adversarial discrepancy audit](/home/amir/Codes/PDE/studies/mean_field_peeling/temporary_loss_gradient_flow_audit/LOSS_PAIRED_DISCREPANCY_AUDIT.md)\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]}]}"
